WEBVTT

05:07.150 --> 05:10.780
All right. I think we're good. If everybody is good,

05:11.280 --> 05:14.340
we're going to go ahead and get started, and we'll call this meeting to order.

05:14.840 --> 05:18.220
It is the budget work session for the City Council, and it

05:18.720 --> 05:21.240
is 3:06 when we are starting.

05:23.720 --> 05:27.260
First of all, I wanted to excuse Councilmember Wood and

05:27.760 --> 05:31.780
Councilmember Hewish. Councilmember Hewish is online, and I think Councilmember

05:32.280 --> 05:35.520
Wood may be joining us if he gets a chance, but we're not sure.

05:36.303 --> 05:36.886
So for right now,

05:36.999 --> 05:39.962
we'll go with that.

05:40.820 --> 05:44.188
And then the first item is a discussion

05:44.528 --> 05:46.861
of the property tax impact schedule.

05:51.693 --> 05:55.282
So I'm deciding who— Kimberly,

05:55.298 --> 05:58.870
did you have some information on this one? I did not go over this agenda

05:59.370 --> 06:01.651
very well, so I'm not as prepared as I need to be. That's why I'm

06:02.151 --> 06:04.443
a little scattered. So,

06:07.532 --> 06:12.431
um, I know we've had conversations about the property tax schedule, but yes.

06:12.528 --> 06:16.121
So, um, Mayor and Council, because we are in the truth in taxation process,

06:16.720 --> 06:20.682
any meeting that we hold in which we will be discussing the 2027

06:21.182 --> 06:24.651
proposed budget, we have to include this, um, property tax

06:25.151 --> 06:28.266
schedule notice. So that's why it is on the agenda.

06:28.362 --> 06:31.929
So, Mayor, what we will have you do is just actually read the

06:32.429 --> 06:35.301
schedule. Um,

06:36.473 --> 06:40.792
yep. Yes. Okay, so we will have that this meeting,

06:41.065 --> 06:44.276
and then we'll have it again. Right now, the next time we would talk budget

06:44.776 --> 06:47.359
is on the 16th, so we've got it scheduled for the 16th as well.

06:48.756 --> 06:51.422
Okay, perfect. That's what I was looking for, and it was just underneath my other

06:51.922 --> 06:55.677
schedule. So I can read this tax pro— the property tax

06:56.177 --> 07:00.159
impact schedule. And then there's no action, just— it's just

07:00.659 --> 07:04.554
information only. Okay. Proposed property tax

07:05.054 --> 07:08.202
impact schedule. The Eagle Mountain City will consider an increase to its property tax rate

07:08.702 --> 07:09.954
to generate an additional $6,958,141.

07:13.923 --> 07:17.352
The following information is intended to provide decision makers

07:18.173 --> 07:21.941
and the public with an explanation of how city's operations would be affected

07:22.118 --> 07:25.758
if the property tax increase is adopted. Eagle Mountain's current

07:26.258 --> 07:30.451
property tax rate is 0.000534.

07:31.286 --> 07:36.763
Eagle Mountain's current property tax revenue is $2,839,998.

07:39.680 --> 07:42.226
The proposed revenue with tax change will be $9,798,139,

07:45.940 --> 07:48.894
and the new property tax revenue to Eagle Mountain City would be $6,958,141.

07:52.324 --> 07:55.669
The estimated increase to Eagle Mountain property tax revenue is

07:55.701 --> 07:59.175
245%. Estimated increase to

07:59.690 --> 08:03.439
average primary resident— estimated increase to average

08:03.939 --> 08:07.464
primary resident market value $500,000, estimated tax increase

08:07.964 --> 08:11.178
would be $359.79, and total city

08:11.243 --> 08:13.668
tax would be $506.64.

08:15.803 --> 08:19.768
The estimated increase to the average business for the market value is $2.5

08:20.268 --> 08:20.825
million— it's $2,554,303.

08:23.562 --> 08:26.174
The estimated tax increase would be $4,705.86.

08:29.952 --> 08:34.032
The affected budget, uh, Sheriff's contract, the proposed budget

08:34.243 --> 08:34.584
is, uh,

08:36.388 --> 08:40.438
$9,798,139,

08:41.600 --> 08:45.715
and then the budget tax change

08:46.215 --> 08:47.213
would be $8,290,000.

08:50.129 --> 08:54.150
Which is $1,507,939

08:54.570 --> 08:57.738
in the budget change. So— sorry,

08:58.158 --> 09:02.356
I've said that wrong. The budget without a tax change— the new change

09:02.856 --> 09:06.275
is the $1 million. If new property tax increase is approved,

09:06.291 --> 09:10.260
the revenue will be used to fund essential public safety operations

09:11.336 --> 09:15.058
at the level approved by the city council. These funds help support core

09:15.558 --> 09:19.518
functions of the Utah County Sheriff's Office Eagle Mountain Division including

09:20.119 --> 09:23.334
the additional— the addition of deputies to meet the growth of the city.

09:25.706 --> 09:28.150
I know that was awesome, but was that clear?

09:29.767 --> 09:34.039
Okay, that is the, uh, tax schedule

09:34.864 --> 09:37.534
that we're required to read. So any questions?

09:38.844 --> 09:42.855
Okay, next item 2B is a budget discussion,

09:43.130 --> 09:46.391
and this for the The proposal of this budget work meeting is for

09:46.891 --> 09:49.665
the City Council to review, discuss, and ask questions about the tentative budget.

09:50.114 --> 09:53.726
Last Tuesday we turned over the tentative budget to

09:54.226 --> 09:57.305
the council, and I think everybody's had

09:57.337 --> 10:00.724
a chance to review that. So now's our chance to

10:00.756 --> 10:04.062
talk about it, and we can open the floor to the council for any questions

10:04.897 --> 10:07.031
that you may have or discussion items.

10:08.026 --> 10:10.996
And as a same thing, I'm sure this could go to anybody, but do you

10:11.496 --> 10:14.569
have any presentation to start with? So it's whatever questions you want to

10:15.069 --> 10:19.107
start with and whoever wants to be brave.

10:21.362 --> 10:25.299
I had Kimberly send out a list of some of the proposals earlier

10:25.799 --> 10:28.256
today. And when I say I, I mean Ben.

10:30.506 --> 10:34.242
And it's stuff that we've talked about

10:34.467 --> 10:37.687
and whatnot, but— I think that

10:38.187 --> 10:41.970
it might be useful if Kimberly or Ben or a staff member,

10:42.470 --> 10:46.470
could just in a very succinct, quick timeline

10:46.970 --> 10:50.890
tell the residents how our process includes reviewing

10:51.390 --> 10:55.110
each department, staffing needs, those kinds of things,

10:55.610 --> 10:59.710
and how we make sure that we're not overspending and we

11:00.210 --> 11:04.230
cut out the fat. Because some of the questions that I see us asked either

11:04.730 --> 11:08.790
on social media or via email or text often include, why don't

11:09.290 --> 11:12.621
you just cut things? And I think that it would be useful

11:13.171 --> 11:16.646
to have someone speak about that so that it's included

11:17.146 --> 11:19.979
as part of this meeting. So if residents go back later and watch it,

11:20.091 --> 11:23.455
they're like, oh, okay, that is something that is a part of their process.

11:27.274 --> 11:30.415
Look, we just need to get her a little table right

11:30.915 --> 11:31.144
up by the podium.

11:38.050 --> 11:41.591
I know Steve offered earlier, hey, do you need to sit over here?

11:41.639 --> 11:44.379
And I says, nope, I'm just going to direct all the questions your way.

11:44.940 --> 11:46.030
So, um, um,

11:46.847 --> 11:50.148
actually, it's my pleasure to

11:50.648 --> 11:53.642
come and to be able to try to address any questions that the council has

11:53.738 --> 11:57.696
regarding this tentative budget process and where we are, um, in that,

11:57.984 --> 12:00.921
um, today. So for the benefit,

12:01.114 --> 12:04.647
and I know Councilmember Clark, you asked for succinct, so that's a challenge for

12:05.147 --> 12:08.003
me. So we'll see if I can stand up and meet that obligation.

12:08.357 --> 12:11.649
It's a challenge for all of us sitting up here on the

12:12.149 --> 12:17.157
dais. So the process for starting preparations

12:17.173 --> 12:20.706
for the fiscal year 2027 budget actually started in October

12:21.206 --> 12:24.271
for us. So there is a— the process

12:24.319 --> 12:27.722
starts internally where we We start meeting with the departments.

12:27.803 --> 12:31.433
We talk about what we call the 5-year needs assessment where we're kind of

12:31.933 --> 12:35.707
reviewing what their expected needs are as we're growing as a community. We look

12:35.788 --> 12:39.065
at their current levels

12:39.113 --> 12:43.966
of services that they are providing and what those— whether

12:44.466 --> 12:47.059
or not we feel like that is continuing to decline. Are we meeting that?

12:47.319 --> 12:50.553
Part of that is the discussion that leads up towards the request.

12:51.525 --> 12:55.301
We look at the challenges that they're facing as a department when it comes to

12:55.801 --> 12:58.927
personnel and particularly when we get to that part of the budget request.

12:59.890 --> 13:03.342
We have what we call a personnel justification where we

13:03.486 --> 13:06.922
ask numerous questions of those divisions when they're requesting

13:07.018 --> 13:10.470
personnel about what the needs are. How would they mitigate it if we can't

13:10.970 --> 13:14.275
approve this? What— we want to make sure that we're capturing what the impacts are

13:14.484 --> 13:17.855
of if we don't say yes or we can't find the resources to

13:18.355 --> 13:21.628
fund this position, what are the things that we need to be aware

13:22.128 --> 13:25.658
of about the level of service that we can continue to provide to the community

13:25.722 --> 13:30.100
as a result of that. As those come in and are

13:30.308 --> 13:33.546
submitted, then we have a committee. Right now

13:33.659 --> 13:36.833
that committee involves myself and Terrence and the

13:36.977 --> 13:40.808
city manager, and we even had the mayor in those— many of

13:41.308 --> 13:44.784
those meetings to be able to participate. But what we start looking at is those

13:44.896 --> 13:48.647
justifications that are coming in and do our internal review process of

13:49.147 --> 13:52.670
where we're trying to again be— make sure

13:53.170 --> 13:56.614
that we are being as unbiased as possible, that because the staffer might be better

13:56.662 --> 14:00.220
at writing their justification or doing their their communication, we don't want

14:00.720 --> 14:03.603
that to necessarily be the sole indicator. We're looking at,

14:03.878 --> 14:08.509
this is for the strategic objectives of this city, of what we are

14:08.557 --> 14:12.028
hoping to accomplish. If we don't fund these positions,

14:12.045 --> 14:15.418
again, what are the risks to— are there safety components?

14:16.130 --> 14:20.190
Are there— oh, I don't know, any number of

14:20.690 --> 14:24.008
items that we have. So there was some preliminary scoring that took place internally that

14:24.057 --> 14:28.310
helped us rank what we classified as the

14:28.390 --> 14:31.748
items that we'd be recommending initially to be able to come forward. One of those

14:31.925 --> 14:35.451
also happens to look at funding and the resources. How can this be funded?

14:36.439 --> 14:40.389
Some areas, if they are covered by individual fees and services,

14:40.486 --> 14:44.359
we might be in a better position to be able to support those

14:45.510 --> 14:47.667
positions with the resources that we have to us.

14:49.592 --> 14:53.072
And then March— I believe it was our March meeting with

14:53.572 --> 14:56.955
the councils when we first initially went over those lists with the with the council

14:57.067 --> 15:00.257
about, we cover to you all of the requested positions,

15:00.289 --> 15:04.265
even the ones that might be recommended as hold at the time as

15:04.297 --> 15:08.144
we bring the tentative budget forward. And then the list that was being kind

15:08.644 --> 15:12.921
of referred to that I resent out is a list that helps identify again of

15:13.354 --> 15:16.912
the positions that are included in the tentative budget, how are they

15:17.201 --> 15:20.952
funded, and what is the individual

15:21.032 --> 15:24.724
impact from a from a funding resource that we have.

15:24.902 --> 15:28.223
So as far as positions go, that's how we got to where we

15:28.723 --> 15:30.459
are today. That's awesome. Thank you so much.

15:33.372 --> 15:35.509
If you want to start, I actually had a couple of things that I wanted

15:35.525 --> 15:39.060
to just throw out there too to think about. And I

15:39.560 --> 15:42.902
can start with this because it's, um, maybe not what the bulk of the meeting

15:43.179 --> 15:46.789
would be about. But with, uh, first of all,

15:46.838 --> 15:50.040
I want to say our staff did a really, really good job. And one thing

15:50.540 --> 15:54.040
I love about Eagle Mountain and what Councilmember Clark kind of touched on,

15:55.650 --> 15:58.640
I see in other cities where it's kind of like use it or lose it

15:59.140 --> 16:02.220
and they just spend just to spend because they want— they don't want their budgets

16:02.720 --> 16:05.620
to go down. And what I'm getting at is here in Eagle Mountain, our staff

16:06.120 --> 16:09.360
does a really, really good job at getting by with what they need. And if

16:09.860 --> 16:13.500
there's extra, you know, it stays in the general fund, we're able to reallocate it

16:14.000 --> 16:16.670
or use it. So I just wanted to point out that they have been absolutely

16:17.813 --> 16:21.470
very, very good at not just wasting

16:21.519 --> 16:25.032
money just because it's there. And I say that and leave

16:25.532 --> 16:29.379
with that for this reason. One of the easiest areas in the past to maybe

16:30.005 --> 16:34.465
cut fat, if you will, or to non-necessary

16:34.802 --> 16:37.946
would be in our events, which we've advocated for a lot.

16:38.331 --> 16:42.005
And I've been one of the biggest critics, but I've also been

16:42.021 --> 16:45.163
one of the biggest advocates of bringing our community together.

16:45.868 --> 16:50.274
And our wonderful events department gave back $30,000

16:50.290 --> 16:54.215
this year from next year's budget compared to last year. So one

16:54.715 --> 16:57.820
of the reasons is we're not doing the Techno Blast. Is that— they say it

16:58.320 --> 17:02.146
right? And, uh, that freed up some space.

17:02.690 --> 17:06.167
One of my questions to the council is, do we want to leave a little

17:06.215 --> 17:09.691
bit in there for either different events that you had ideas,

17:09.820 --> 17:13.168
uh, on, or one of the things that I know I've talked to council members,

17:13.200 --> 17:16.472
the Easter egg hunt was just an Easter egg hunt. It's kind of

17:16.972 --> 17:20.596
over in just a few minutes. Do we maybe bolster that with

17:20.725 --> 17:24.287
other activities and leave a little bit of money in there so

17:24.351 --> 17:27.721
that event can be, you know, a couple hours with some booths

17:28.221 --> 17:31.332
and face painting or whatever that is? But I think all those things cost money.

17:31.477 --> 17:35.200
So maybe it's half that money gets left in there

17:35.489 --> 17:39.372
or whatever that is. I'm just telling you that they were doing

17:39.872 --> 17:44.588
their part to be very, very very good with their finances,

17:44.636 --> 17:48.408
and I think we could feel confident that if we left

17:48.908 --> 17:52.036
a cushion in there that it wouldn't get wasted. It would just be there so

17:52.536 --> 17:55.728
that if we need it, it's there. That's, uh, why I

17:56.228 --> 18:00.045
mentioned the first part. The other thing is, um, we did— I

18:00.206 --> 18:03.881
wanted to report back that the Nolan Park $750,000

18:03.898 --> 18:07.043
has been allocated from last year and it didn't

18:07.543 --> 18:11.535
get spent this year. So the Nolan Park discussion There's money already

18:12.035 --> 18:15.222
set aside, and do we want to either add to that?

18:16.008 --> 18:19.807
We had a big discussion that led right up to this meeting about, you know,

18:19.951 --> 18:23.558
some design. What does that park look like

18:24.058 --> 18:27.485
in the future? And I'm just going to be honest, I don't know. And we—

18:27.822 --> 18:31.092
none of us know. That's why we need to— but what are we willing to—

18:31.573 --> 18:35.244
there's $750,000 that's already in there that's depreciating every

18:35.744 --> 18:37.529
time we're not using it. So what do we want to do with that?

18:37.628 --> 18:41.778
So those are some of the questions. Another one that I've

18:42.278 --> 18:44.746
put out to a couple people is the North Service Area. If we start allocating

18:44.843 --> 18:48.554
funds to Salt Shed or something up there, that's another budget

18:49.054 --> 18:51.944
that came in under budget, was our streets department.

18:53.600 --> 18:57.138
So they always have the ability to do more maintenance on years

18:57.154 --> 19:00.629
that we have less snow. So we don't discount that, but just things to—

19:01.578 --> 19:05.937
I want those things on your radar and more field

19:06.437 --> 19:07.863
or better, you know, maybe some repairs in our fields with taking on the soccer.

19:08.493 --> 19:11.868
So I'm throwing out some of those capital and other projects

19:12.368 --> 19:15.631
as well as the events budget for you guys to have on your radar.

19:16.795 --> 19:22.506
And then I know that we're gonna probably dive into some employee

19:22.700 --> 19:25.874
asks and needs, I guess, as we grow and

19:26.374 --> 19:29.820
need more staff members. So I'm just leading with some of those

19:29.918 --> 19:33.751
things. They're not in any particular order. I don't have any preference,

19:37.446 --> 19:41.273
just whatever you guys want to— this is our time to move

19:45.826 --> 19:49.586
So one question I have, just looking at the list of positions

19:49.715 --> 19:53.523
that Kimberly sent us earlier today, I see a position

19:53.636 --> 19:57.011
not there that was on a list earlier that we'd received

19:57.028 --> 19:59.418
from Terrence pertaining to the library.

20:00.864 --> 20:04.206
And I'm curious to know why the new list that we got didn't have anything

20:04.399 --> 20:08.085
from the library on that. So the list that we sent

20:08.585 --> 20:12.068
to you after— so initially went out on May 6th— is because

20:12.568 --> 20:15.613
this is the list of positions that are included in the tentative budget.

20:17.496 --> 20:20.630
That's what's on this list. So it does not include

20:20.646 --> 20:24.359
any of the positions that had been originally requested that are

20:24.407 --> 20:27.778
not recommended moving forward in the budget. Okay,

20:27.826 --> 20:31.536
so the— so there was, there was a list of original requests

20:32.243 --> 20:35.888
and then it got filtered is what you're saying, and who did the filtering

20:35.936 --> 20:39.807
process? So the filtering process comes through

20:40.674 --> 20:43.613
the budgetary committee side of things, so that would be Ben,

20:43.822 --> 20:47.226
myself, the mayor, of identifying

20:48.206 --> 20:51.322
what we have in terms of funding resources and

20:51.354 --> 20:54.951
when we're looking at what we need, what is being recommended to come forward

20:55.031 --> 20:58.258
as part of that tentative budget administration request.

20:59.140 --> 21:02.591
Okay. Is it okay for me to speak on the library's behalf for

21:03.091 --> 21:05.816
a moment? Is that appropriate here? And it may be good to have the library

21:06.316 --> 21:10.101
speak too. I know Michelle is attending online, but from what I understand

21:10.278 --> 21:13.551
is our library is understaffed by something

21:13.583 --> 21:16.921
like 8 or 9 employees.

21:18.157 --> 21:21.014
And so given that there's been astronomical growth,

21:21.752 --> 21:24.953
especially recently, in our library subscriptions,

21:26.016 --> 21:28.707
and I know the library had put forward, at least originally,

21:29.999 --> 21:32.589
a, I think, one full-time employee,

21:32.929 --> 21:37.105
which doesn't even remotely get them close to the number they should have according

21:37.202 --> 21:40.309
to what standards are. And so that's why I was just curious

21:40.809 --> 21:44.528
as to why that particular position didn't make it across the finish

21:45.028 --> 21:48.455
line. And perhaps, you know, it may be good for the council to

21:48.488 --> 21:50.588
hear from some thoughts from Michelle on that as well.

21:53.032 --> 21:56.428
Um, certainly Michelle can absolutely speak to this as well. I think

21:56.928 --> 22:00.148
our biggest deficiency is not necessarily in staffing of the library, it's in the square

22:00.648 --> 22:03.779
footage of the library. And, and that's our,

22:03.924 --> 22:07.248
our biggest challenge as our city has continued to grow. And you look at our

22:07.748 --> 22:10.720
square footage and the, and the number of books and periodicals and everything else that

22:11.220 --> 22:14.859
we have to offer our residents, it's dramatically low. And as we

22:15.359 --> 22:18.724
plan for a new city hall and plan for a new library Certainly we're going

22:18.741 --> 22:22.190
to need to staff up at that point to be able to take care of

22:22.223 --> 22:25.996
those needs at that time. And so I think from an administration point of view,

22:26.028 --> 22:29.854
we're really looking at it from the space constraints that we have and

22:29.967 --> 22:33.659
when is it the most appropriate to add staff and would that be in

22:33.755 --> 22:35.045
the planning of a new facility.

22:37.988 --> 22:41.551
If I may, Councilmember Whiting, too, I do want to clarify and

22:41.729 --> 22:45.427
make sure that even from our staff standpoint, there's a level of

22:45.927 --> 22:49.035
understanding that there was only a couple, a handful of

22:49.535 --> 22:52.307
positions that maybe didn't make it through. And then again, of the list we said,

22:52.323 --> 22:56.091
these are what we would call a P1, P2, meaning like the P2 positions

22:56.591 --> 23:00.293
were ones that were initially also flagged as being on hold again from funding resources.

23:00.357 --> 23:04.414
But every position that is asked for is desperately

23:04.447 --> 23:08.023
needed within the individual departments. So one of the things

23:08.071 --> 23:11.760
that I would like to stress that we do differently at Eagle Mountain

23:12.260 --> 23:15.919
City is when we're doing our budget process, we are building on. So the

23:16.419 --> 23:19.371
initial department requests that are submitted for the budget,

23:20.158 --> 23:23.353
our departments are really good at knowing this isn't an

23:23.530 --> 23:26.484
ask of, if we could have everything that we wanted,

23:26.821 --> 23:30.675
here's what we're going to ask for. We start off with,

23:30.691 --> 23:33.870
what does it take to just do next year exactly what we're doing

23:33.966 --> 23:37.290
this year? And if we don't have the money to keep doing that, we don't

23:37.790 --> 23:41.388
get to add anything more to that. The first conversation is,

23:41.453 --> 23:45.073
again, we have a revenue resource problem and we can't do anything more unless

23:45.573 --> 23:49.166
we can get additional revenue. And then if that happens, then we can

23:49.198 --> 23:52.694
start to add in some of these additional asks that we have or that are

23:53.194 --> 23:56.960
needed. But at Eagle Mountain City, we will always pace behind

23:57.460 --> 24:00.425
simply as a level of the growth that we have. And we do have a

24:00.925 --> 24:04.390
lot of unique things that we, um,

24:04.406 --> 24:07.214
just based on the layout that we have of our city and so forth,

24:07.311 --> 24:10.775
of the infrastructure that has to connect across open spaces and

24:11.275 --> 24:14.770
regions that we have. We are 3 cities that are interconnected

24:14.866 --> 24:17.721
by these infrastructure needs.

24:18.074 --> 24:21.491
We have a lot of demanding growth and so forth. I'm not telling you anything

24:21.539 --> 24:24.490
that you don't need to— that you don't already know, so I want to make

24:24.990 --> 24:28.324
sure I'm not just talking to talk. What I wanted to stress here is that

24:28.982 --> 24:32.816
our departments, of all the requests— if you want a list

24:33.316 --> 24:36.473
of all the requests that everybody needs, we don't submit though. They don't even ask

24:36.505 --> 24:39.793
for it in the department, in, in our budget, because they know it's not even

24:39.825 --> 24:43.756
an option. These aren't what it takes us to get to what we would consider

24:44.256 --> 24:47.354
to be an optimal level. What we're getting is this is the minimum we feel

24:47.854 --> 24:50.940
like we can do to try to keep pulsing forward and not necessarily be going

24:50.974 --> 24:54.061
backwards. I think that's what I was

24:54.174 --> 24:57.631
going to point out, to be really clear with

24:58.131 --> 25:01.508
the council, is the, the budget committee that

25:02.008 --> 25:06.082
I've been a part of has never ever just

25:06.195 --> 25:09.621
cut a position. It's always the— on the department head or something like,

25:09.670 --> 25:12.890
hey, who What can we get by with and what can't we?

25:13.390 --> 25:16.890
Nobody's ever said, well, you can't have that. It's usually the department head saying,

25:17.390 --> 25:20.650
well, I need 10 guys, but I'll get by with 7.

25:21.150 --> 25:23.830
It's never came from the other side.

25:24.210 --> 25:27.790
So I've personally never told anybody they couldn't ask for a position,

25:28.290 --> 25:31.930
and I don't think Ben has, I don't think Kimberly has. So when those get

25:32.430 --> 25:35.470
moved to a different list like that, it's because the department head, and I think

25:35.970 --> 25:39.274
Ben nailed it, is space. If we hired 7 more people, where would you put

25:39.774 --> 25:42.101
them? I think there would be more than the patrons there because we can't fit

25:42.601 --> 25:45.989
that many people in the building, right? But could they use—

25:46.359 --> 25:48.228
do we have the work for 7 more people? Yes.

25:49.824 --> 25:53.631
But can we get by with 1 or

25:54.131 --> 25:57.418
none? They also said yes. And I would maybe add to

25:57.918 --> 26:01.271
that that the state standard is that we should have more libraries. There's no doubt,

26:01.547 --> 26:04.745
per our population. But we should also have more square footage

26:04.794 --> 26:08.581
for our population, more articles to check out for our population.

26:08.856 --> 26:12.335
So it's one component of many. Mayor Zach has a comment

26:12.835 --> 26:16.408
online. Who did you say? Zach? Zach? Yeah,

26:16.555 --> 26:19.031
I told him that he'd come to him right after Brad, but we can go

26:19.531 --> 26:22.550
there now if you're done. Yeah, go ahead, Zach. We'll come to you now.

26:24.558 --> 26:27.789
Thank you. I apologize for not being there in person.

26:27.838 --> 26:28.461
Can you hear me okay?

26:31.170 --> 26:34.204
He's— can you hear me? We can't hear you, Zach, if you're talking.

26:37.722 --> 26:41.127
Um, I'm unmuted. Something's not working.

26:43.808 --> 26:44.854
So is it a volume?

26:51.520 --> 26:54.677
Let's go. I'm going to jump to Brett real quick. Sorry, Council Member

26:55.177 --> 26:57.590
Wright had some comments, and we'll come back to you in a second. Zach,

26:58.090 --> 26:59.810
see if we can get it figured out. You can call me Brett Mayer.

27:00.310 --> 27:04.130
That's my first name. My mom calls me Brent and she named me, so as

27:04.630 --> 27:06.740
long as you don't call me Brent, that'll be fine.

27:08.810 --> 27:11.370
Yeah, just a couple of thoughts. I mean, as I've kind of gone through the

27:11.870 --> 27:14.050
budget, I think that the budget team's done a really good job with this budget

27:14.550 --> 27:17.830
this year. Let me just start there. A couple of things that

27:18.330 --> 27:21.670
I'd— and we'll come back and maybe talk about personnel. Looks like Zach may be

27:22.170 --> 27:26.720
there. Something happened.

27:27.220 --> 27:30.060
Okay. All right, thank you. I think,

27:30.560 --> 27:34.220
I mean, I think we should, I think of events, we're able to save the

27:34.720 --> 27:37.560
$30,000 that was in their budget last year. I think we should allocate that,

27:38.060 --> 27:39.860
appropriate that to them so that they can hang on to it. And if they

27:40.360 --> 27:42.840
need to expand some of the events that we're currently doing to make them a

27:43.340 --> 27:45.410
little bit better and a little broader, I would be in favor of that.

27:47.400 --> 27:49.740
The streets department, you and I talked a little bit about this. I don't know

27:50.240 --> 27:54.170
what their budget savings was this year, but For me, I think it makes complete

27:54.670 --> 27:58.090
sense to build a lean-to and put some salt up on the north service

27:58.590 --> 28:00.870
area. I think that'll save us a bunch of fuel costs, and maybe let's park

28:01.370 --> 28:04.550
one of our snowplows up there. Something. We ought to consider that where

28:05.050 --> 28:07.760
we already own the property. That's something I would be supportive of as well.

28:08.310 --> 28:11.650
I really want to see us program and start to program or allocate

28:12.150 --> 28:15.990
some resources for a park in the Sweetwater area. I know that Parkway Fields is

28:16.490 --> 28:20.108
working on their thing. I think there's a 10-acre space there, and Brad,

28:20.284 --> 28:23.798
I think you know where I'm talking about. I don't know if council would support

28:24.298 --> 28:28.113
that, but I, I just feel like with the work that we've done in,

28:28.531 --> 28:32.477
in the ranch side of things where we did the, the Smith Ranch Park,

28:32.638 --> 28:36.055
it's probably the most recent big park that we put a lot of money

28:36.087 --> 28:39.183
into. I'd really like to see us do something similar in the city

28:39.311 --> 28:43.114
center section, and I think that's a really good area where we have some missing,

28:44.028 --> 28:47.700
perhaps a need. Can I give you an update? Sure. So, so I

28:48.200 --> 28:50.720
forgot to circle back with you, and we talked about this as far as,

28:51.220 --> 28:54.900
you know, different areas. And two things that I wanted to make sure that

28:55.400 --> 28:58.060
I, I forgot, and it's my fault. Number one is the Nolan Park already had

28:58.560 --> 29:02.220
the 750 in there. I know that. And then the number two part was the

29:02.720 --> 29:06.920
observatory. We are spending a ton of money, not in that park, but the

29:07.420 --> 29:10.680
observatory. And, and most of that is donated. But the update

29:11.180 --> 29:14.120
that I wanted to get out is that we did get. Do you want to

29:14.620 --> 29:17.820
speak to it, or you want me to? Is there an ask from council though

29:18.320 --> 29:20.570
to match some funds or to set some funds aside for that observatory?

29:21.800 --> 29:25.400
I think that's the update. We, um, we— when I say we,

29:25.900 --> 29:28.860
I mean Natalie, uh, reached out and, um,

29:29.360 --> 29:33.300
presented to the, the county for transportation funds. We did get a $250,000 award

29:33.800 --> 29:37.460
from that just recently, so we're shy of about, about $250,000 to

29:37.960 --> 29:41.260
get that project funded, but it's really close. And that's a total of

29:41.760 --> 29:44.900
about $1.5 million, a little more than that. Yeah,

29:44.990 --> 29:48.410
$1.5 million that will be spent here in City Center on the observatory.

29:48.960 --> 29:52.900
And that's everything. We're within $200,000 getting— $250,000

29:53.400 --> 29:56.840
getting that funded. So to your point, I think that,

29:57.430 --> 30:01.070
that I'm going to work on a grant for that $250,000.

30:01.660 --> 30:06.060
In the meantime, if the— if we don't get other funds You

30:06.560 --> 30:11.693
know, I think that's something we need to talk about on top of Navy.

30:11.790 --> 30:15.705
What's the anticipated M&amp;O on that, Mayor? I'm just curious what we're looking

30:15.754 --> 30:17.873
at to take care of that long term.

30:18.036 --> 30:21.279
Natalie, you better speak to that because I know she has

30:21.974 --> 30:27.340
some— there will be a way to check those out. And I

30:27.840 --> 30:30.611
think for the most part it's not a ton, but a lot of it is

30:30.789 --> 30:34.814
having the building so that the Utah Valley Astronomy

30:34.910 --> 30:38.433
Club can come out and like co-host events with the city

30:38.465 --> 30:42.464
or local organizations. And Natalie will say more, but like

30:42.530 --> 30:45.688
I was a part of this when it was first proposed,

30:46.680 --> 30:50.110
and the nerds— I'm going to say that

30:50.158 --> 30:53.557
as a term of endearment— will do a lot of the,

30:54.040 --> 30:57.633
the stuff, the programming that takes place. Ours is really

30:57.792 --> 31:01.088
the charge of taking care of the physical facility, which is

31:01.169 --> 31:02.607
like a small building.

31:04.445 --> 31:07.198
So— And sorry, I only heard part of your question. You said, what is the

31:07.698 --> 31:11.236
anticipated what? I guess if you could just speak to what

31:11.736 --> 31:15.779
the maintenance and operation expenses are going to be related

31:16.279 --> 31:18.333
to it once it's in place. If we can find the money to do it,

31:18.365 --> 31:21.499
what's it going to cost us to take care of it? So we're expecting very

31:21.596 --> 31:25.245
minimal maintenance costs. Um, it— like,

31:25.630 --> 31:29.589
um, Councilmember Clark mentioned, it is like a standalone structure.

31:29.653 --> 31:32.939
There is no restroom, so we're already maintaining the park

31:33.439 --> 31:36.755
restroom at that park, and that's the same restroom we intend to have.

31:36.931 --> 31:40.682
You know, it'll be right by each other, so we're already maintaining

31:40.714 --> 31:44.337
that. We're already maintaining other features at the park. Um, we'll just

31:44.385 --> 31:47.575
make sure it stays clean, and then, um,

31:47.831 --> 31:51.860
UVU will help us with the maintenance of the actual telescopes themselves

31:52.360 --> 31:55.560
because they are the high-expert— that's what I was concerned about is who's going to

31:56.060 --> 31:58.660
take care of that, what— how is that going to fall, uh, who's that going

31:59.160 --> 32:01.600
to fall upon, and are we— I don't know how to take care of a

32:02.100 --> 32:04.880
telescope. I don't know if our staff knows how to do that, but I'm just

32:05.380 --> 32:08.100
concerned about the nerds. The nerds will come do it. Okay,

32:08.600 --> 32:11.400
well, that's all right. I was part of the nerd club at one point.

32:11.540 --> 32:15.440
I'm still part of the nerd club, I think. Councilmember Clark,

32:15.940 --> 32:18.600
that, that answers That answers my question, Natalie. Thank you. And I don't want you

32:19.100 --> 32:21.920
to think that takes away from Parkway Fields. I just forgot to tell you that—

32:22.420 --> 32:25.980
from Sweetwater. First Sweetwater. Yeah, that there are funds being allocated

32:26.480 --> 32:29.680
throughout the city. I'm just hoping that we can

32:30.180 --> 32:33.280
find a way to program some impact fees for that park and see that advance.

32:33.780 --> 32:37.800
That's what I'm hoping. So I'm going to advocate for that. And then I'll

32:38.300 --> 32:41.720
try and be quick and give everybody else a chance to speak. One of the

32:42.220 --> 32:46.285
other things that we've talked about ad nauseam at least from my perspective, is how

32:46.349 --> 32:50.137
are we going to slow speeds down at our elementary schools? And I know that

32:50.202 --> 32:53.460
not everyone's going to like to hear this or agree with me, but I'm going

32:53.476 --> 32:55.852
to put it out there. Councilmember Wood and I have been working on this.

32:55.964 --> 32:59.929
In fact, David Salazar did a little bit of work for

33:00.073 --> 33:03.605
us to give us some idea of what speed tables might cost

33:04.105 --> 33:06.350
to improve some of these areas by our elementary schools.

33:07.216 --> 33:11.293
And a rough estimate to do it in concrete is somewhere between $8,000

33:11.793 --> 33:15.003
and $10,000 per retrofit. And I just think it's far past time for us to

33:15.503 --> 33:18.135
do that and figure out a way to plow around them or over them.

33:19.512 --> 33:22.711
I think we need to do that for Silver Lake, for Brookhaven, for Mountain Trails,

33:22.776 --> 33:25.033
for Desert Sky. There may be others.

33:25.480 --> 33:29.246
Perhaps the school that's just, just directly south of us here

33:29.311 --> 33:32.899
may merit some of that type of traffic mitigation. But I

33:33.110 --> 33:35.864
really want to see us put— if, if you get it done for $8,000 or

33:36.364 --> 33:39.770
$10,000, I think we ought to put $100,000 in the budget, and we ought to

33:40.270 --> 33:42.850
allocate it towards that, and we need to do that sooner rather than later.

33:43.470 --> 33:47.150
That would probably be just the concrete cost. There's also a lot of—

33:47.650 --> 33:50.210
it's a lot more than that. So come tell us, David, what is it going

33:50.710 --> 33:53.030
to cost? What do we need to allocate? Because I'm going to tell you,

33:53.530 --> 33:57.130
from my perspective, it's high time that we do something about this. So I

33:57.630 --> 33:59.820
was texting you while you were talking, hopefully you wouldn't get to that point yet,

34:00.230 --> 34:03.470
but it's probably more than double. For asphalt, it's going to be

34:03.700 --> 34:08.420
anywhere from $15,000 to $17,000. For concrete, probably $28,000

34:08.920 --> 34:12.460
to $30,000. I think concrete's probably the better option. They cut it in and they

34:12.960 --> 34:16.420
kind of fill it in, use the existing asphalt as like a formwork. I think

34:16.920 --> 34:20.140
that's a better product, less likely to wear off than just kind of doing the

34:20.640 --> 34:24.160
asphalt on top of it. I think at least in those 4 locations,

34:24.600 --> 34:27.910
we just, we hear it every, every, every school year.

34:27.960 --> 34:31.506
It'll come back again when school opens next year. We just have people that are

34:32.006 --> 34:35.585
trucking through those neighborhoods at higher speeds. You only do that once on a speed

34:35.601 --> 34:38.475
table, and that will curb behavior, literally.

34:39.599 --> 34:42.730
And I just think it's time that we figure out

34:43.230 --> 34:46.423
a way to do that. I know that our streets department's not fond of that

34:46.923 --> 34:50.180
suggestion or proposal, and I also know that it has

34:50.212 --> 34:53.777
an impact on emergency services, so I'm not numb or blind

34:53.809 --> 34:57.439
to those impacts. But at some point, I think we've got

34:57.939 --> 35:00.999
to take some action to curb some of the, the speeds that we're having in

35:01.499 --> 35:04.219
the elementary schools. Hang on, before we move off of this, I just want to

35:04.719 --> 35:07.869
make sure the, the staff and everybody knows what we're advocating. Do you want $100,000,

35:08.419 --> 35:10.839
or do you want 7, or do you want 4? Like, what's— how much is

35:11.339 --> 35:14.979
it going to cost to do one complete, David? $25 grand.

35:15.479 --> 35:18.199
I think we have to have at least these 4 or 5. So we do

35:18.699 --> 35:21.969
2, 3. I think Silver Lake, Brookhaven, Mountain Trails, it's probably $200,000.

35:22.608 --> 35:25.884
So if we— you want to do a dollar amount and see how far it

35:26.384 --> 35:28.790
gets us? That's what I'd like to do. Well, and it's one-time funds too.

35:28.838 --> 35:32.290
I mean, we could use other sources, but— and that was the other thing that

35:32.790 --> 35:35.662
Councilmember Wood and I have talked about, is perhaps we should use some of the

35:36.162 --> 35:39.772
money from the utility sales to fund this and to just make it

35:40.272 --> 35:44.333
happen. And the thing I would ask you, David, and perhaps staff, is if

35:44.349 --> 35:47.464
there are other areas that we're blind to, that we're not aware of, where we

35:47.964 --> 35:50.638
think that we ought to be considering this? I'd just like to know that.

35:50.750 --> 35:54.307
These are the 4 that I know, because I get complaints about these all the

35:54.807 --> 35:58.121
time. I, I think there are some others we could look at. The comment

35:58.621 --> 36:01.823
I mentioned to both you and, um, Council Member Wood was that I think before

36:02.323 --> 36:05.733
we do this, we need to have code that kind of dictates where these are

36:06.233 --> 36:09.563
applicable and for what reasons. Also, that would limit in spacing,

36:09.675 --> 36:12.977
and then, you know, certain road types where it would not be applicable. Otherwise,

36:13.025 --> 36:15.192
I think we would just open open the floodgates and they would be everywhere,

36:15.209 --> 36:18.358
unfortunately. Sounds like if council agrees, you're giving yourself an assignment.

36:18.567 --> 36:21.652
Sounds like to me. Sounds good to me. Can I ask too,

36:22.584 --> 36:26.103
I'm supportive of us finding some sort of traffic calming

36:26.473 --> 36:30.040
or control measure. I think that is one

36:30.088 --> 36:34.009
of the biggest things that we hear about. I know that in Salt Lake I've

36:34.509 --> 36:38.187
seen that they have the speed tables, but then there's like a cutout

36:38.687 --> 36:42.266
at a certain width for tires, and I'm assuming that the cutout

36:42.766 --> 36:46.313
for the certain width of tires is for emergency vehicles. Does anyone in the

36:46.813 --> 36:50.906
sound of my voice know what I'm talking about? Oh, there's so many— somebody

36:50.954 --> 36:55.130
in the back row. Anyway, um, I, I'm wondering if we

36:55.630 --> 36:59.401
can kind of split the baby when it comes to these speed tables and have

36:59.901 --> 37:03.737
it be something that doesn't impede emergency

37:04.237 --> 37:08.378
services but does get the job accomplished for the speed

37:08.878 --> 37:11.830
table. I don't know why they have those, but I'm assuming it has something to

37:11.846 --> 37:15.201
do with that. But yeah, and we can talk more. If you can send me

37:15.701 --> 37:19.263
more information, I can look into that. Um, I think speed tables is an option,

37:19.777 --> 37:23.085
um, but they're not like this magical mythical thing that's going to fix all the

37:23.585 --> 37:26.649
speed problems. Sometimes they often just move it to another location or another street.

37:26.986 --> 37:29.202
So those are things we have to look at when we're adding these as well.

37:29.411 --> 37:32.911
Yeah, and specifically for me, it's really a safety issue at our elementary schools.

37:33.248 --> 37:37.294
For me, that's the reason I think we need to be looking at that.

37:37.583 --> 37:40.891
Yeah, I think the other one could also be parks potentially

37:41.391 --> 37:44.182
adjacent to busier collectors. We could look at that as well, park crossing.

37:45.210 --> 37:48.421
That's excellent. And I think when smarter people than me get together and start to

37:48.437 --> 37:52.179
have those conversations, we'll figure it out. Okay. So, and then

37:52.997 --> 37:56.723
I'll just quickly run through the positions, and I'm sure we'll have further dialogue

37:57.223 --> 37:59.998
on this, and then, and then I'll be I kind of leave it to the

38:00.498 --> 38:03.723
council. There's a couple of positions that I need to learn more about. Um,

38:04.012 --> 38:08.042
an assistant city attorney. I know that— I know Marcus is overworked

38:08.542 --> 38:12.008
and overwhelmed with much of the work that we're giving him, so I'm not saying

38:12.185 --> 38:15.412
no to that. I want to understand a little bit more of the reasoning behind

38:15.912 --> 38:19.748
that. Seems like we've been using some outside counsel, and maybe it's

38:20.248 --> 38:23.200
just an economy of scale there, so I'd like to get some more information about

38:23.700 --> 38:28.024
that. Equally, I'm interested in hearing more about the payroll and benefits administrator,

38:28.633 --> 38:31.773
trying to understand, is it just a growth issue? We just

38:32.273 --> 38:35.795
have too many people and we don't have enough folks managing that? And then

38:36.295 --> 38:39.865
the one for me that is really kind of a tough pill to swallow,

38:39.945 --> 38:43.470
and let me— and I'll put it out there and I'll tell you the why

38:43.615 --> 38:47.236
behind this— is the community services director. When I look at just

38:47.736 --> 38:52.596
those 3 positions, what I see is I see us adding qualified,

38:52.662 --> 38:54.177
competent leadership.

38:56.923 --> 39:00.492
And my thoughts and feelings are that we need— I think

39:00.541 --> 39:04.174
we need more boots on the ground doing the legwork and

39:04.191 --> 39:06.416
taking care of the actual filling the potholes,

39:07.720 --> 39:11.392
maintaining the grass, preparing the soccer fields.

39:12.084 --> 39:15.192
We haven't even talked about recreation. When we get to recreation, perhaps we can

39:15.692 --> 39:18.768
talk about that because that's a whole new department in my opinion, and we have

39:19.268 --> 39:21.466
to talk about how are we going to manage the soccer program and who's going

39:21.514 --> 39:24.870
to do it and what resources are we going to give to the city to

39:25.370 --> 39:27.840
do that, to the, to the staff to do that. So those are some of

39:28.340 --> 39:32.144
the questions that I have. I'm a little hesitant on those 3 upper-end

39:32.176 --> 39:36.190
positions because I feel like, um, maybe our

39:36.690 --> 39:39.160
focus needs to be more on putting the boots on the ground so that the

39:39.660 --> 39:41.810
work actually gets done. I want to know what are the results that we can

39:41.826 --> 39:44.907
expect by approving these positions in the budget.

39:45.051 --> 39:48.577
That's really what I'm hoping to see is not just that we need these people,

39:48.593 --> 39:52.022
because I believe that, 100% believe that. In fact, I know

39:52.071 --> 39:54.969
that to be true. We need more than the people that are on this list.

39:55.130 --> 39:58.737
I think Kimberly just told us that, that they've already removed people.

39:58.834 --> 40:02.056
But for me, I want to know what's the return on our investment? What can

40:02.556 --> 40:06.576
we expect as a council? Can we see some

40:07.076 --> 40:11.219
results in our utility billing being more effective? And in managing that

40:11.268 --> 40:14.767
department better for our community, for our citizens,

40:15.023 --> 40:17.768
and serving their needs in a little higher way.

40:18.811 --> 40:21.219
I mean, can we expect our soccer fields and our,

40:21.925 --> 40:25.055
our parks to be— to fill in the mole

40:25.071 --> 40:29.003
holes? I don't know what you call them, the, the holes. And, and so that

40:29.503 --> 40:32.678
people aren't tripping and they're not risking some of that stuff, can we manicure them

40:33.178 --> 40:36.525
a little bit better? I don't expect golf course level work, but can we

40:36.573 --> 40:40.010
improve the quality of our fields? And if we're going to do that,

40:40.026 --> 40:43.510
I don't see that as being someone that is in an administrative

40:44.010 --> 40:46.153
position. I see that as being somebody that's out there with a shovel and a

40:46.653 --> 40:50.998
wheelbarrow and some dirt and a rake and some grass seed. That's kind

40:51.498 --> 40:53.107
of where my mindset's at on this budget.

40:56.623 --> 40:58.447
Okay, can we try Zach again?

41:00.335 --> 41:03.569
Can you hear me? They— I hear

41:03.699 --> 41:06.945
that online they can hear him, but it's not in here. We can't hear him.

41:07.025 --> 41:10.401
So you could—

41:10.465 --> 41:13.326
can you hear me now? I see his thing moving, but it's not coming through.

41:14.912 --> 41:18.068
We can call. Can we call?

41:18.165 --> 41:22.337
Can you do that? Okay. Uh, I know Councilman Whiting

41:22.353 --> 41:26.044
had another comment too, so while you're doing that, let's just have a quick 60-second

41:26.076 --> 41:29.653
plug on the, on the the speed tables. I'm 100% in agreement with that.

41:29.846 --> 41:33.137
We need to focus on the safety of our kids in Eagle Mountain.

41:33.217 --> 41:36.540
And that's one of our treasures is we have an amazing kid population.

41:36.604 --> 41:39.976
We got to really prioritize them. And the data I

41:40.476 --> 41:43.845
have to help with this is at my employment, we— so

41:44.214 --> 41:47.730
in Lehi, we— the way our office building

41:47.762 --> 41:51.262
is set up, there is a private road that the office owns,

41:51.775 --> 41:55.307
but it's a public access. And so we have two

41:55.807 --> 41:59.176
parking lots, and in the middle of that is a road privately owned by us

41:59.676 --> 42:03.993
but publicly used by the citizens of Lehigh. They were flying through

42:04.493 --> 42:08.006
there, and it's two parking lots that people were walking back and forth, and it's—

42:09.034 --> 42:13.127
anyway, it was dangerous. We put in a speed bump, a lot

42:13.627 --> 42:16.996
less expensive than a speed table, but the speed bump cost us maybe $7,000.

42:17.783 --> 42:21.819
Solved the traffic issue, the speeding issue. Everybody is now

42:21.933 --> 42:25.605
going really slow there. It works really well, and so I'm 100%

42:25.670 --> 42:27.603
on board with what we need to do for our schools.

42:31.113 --> 42:35.034
I have Zach on the phone. Ready? All right.

42:36.744 --> 42:40.081
Yep, go ahead, Zach. Okay, now I think

42:40.581 --> 42:44.010
so. All right, perfect. Thank you. Well, I appreciate

42:44.590 --> 42:48.391
everyone's comments so far, and I'll just try to Summarize

42:48.487 --> 42:52.093
on some of the, uh, capital projects. The speed tables, I,

42:52.349 --> 42:55.810
I fully support that. I actually went down to

42:56.310 --> 42:58.710
Silver Lake when they were getting out of school and it was just a madhouse

42:59.479 --> 43:03.181
with those two, uh, schools stacked together. It makes sense to slow the

43:03.681 --> 43:06.850
speed down and trim the trees. There are some signs that

43:07.350 --> 43:10.952
you just can't see at all that exist in the park strip that just

43:11.452 --> 43:14.782
either need to be removed or, or cut back. So I would I would agree,

43:14.862 --> 43:17.986
tend to agree with the speed tables. Um,

43:19.172 --> 43:22.713
on the— I've got a, I've got a list of things, but I just wanted

43:23.213 --> 43:27.279
to walk through my process as we were reviewing the

43:27.779 --> 43:31.573
staff, uh, requests that were prioritized as P1

43:32.470 --> 43:35.931
and P2. I initially took the

43:36.431 --> 43:40.241
P1 list, those that had been prioritized first, and sat

43:40.741 --> 43:44.616
down with both the mayor and with Ben and reviewed those positions one by

43:44.665 --> 43:48.196
one. I think we invested about an hour and 45 minutes going

43:48.696 --> 43:51.777
through that. After going through that process, a couple

43:51.873 --> 43:55.902
days, um, later, I received

43:56.402 --> 43:59.836
the second list, and I think Kim mentioned that she had forwarded

44:00.336 --> 44:03.849
the P2s which were being advanced. The first

44:04.349 --> 44:08.280
round, the cost, I believe, was about $1 million of staff

44:08.328 --> 44:12.676
that we were being asked to consider. And with the second

44:12.692 --> 44:18.764
additional round of staff, it added another $864,000

44:18.893 --> 44:22.274
of staffing costs. One of the things that I

44:22.306 --> 44:25.735
would caution the council against is using RAP

44:26.235 --> 44:29.989
tax dollars that was included as a potential source

44:30.489 --> 44:34.307
of revenue to pay for open space people. And I

44:34.420 --> 44:38.926
would just encourage the council to not create

44:39.054 --> 44:43.232
a RAP tax that— or create salaries that

44:43.732 --> 44:48.021
are dependent on that RAP tax being available. So that was one principle that I

44:48.213 --> 44:51.427
really don't support, using the RAP tax to

44:51.927 --> 44:55.541
pay for salaries, although I understand it goes to pay, you know,

44:55.605 --> 44:59.879
someone's salary to maintain the parks. I really feel like that needs to be maintained

45:00.379 --> 45:07.055
through other revenue sources. So my mindset

45:07.555 --> 45:10.872
around the first cost of $1 million versus $1.8 million,

45:12.031 --> 45:15.727
in my mind, I think it would be prudent to work

45:16.227 --> 45:18.875
with the first million-dollar amount,

45:19.536 --> 45:21.677
whether they're P1 or P2,

45:22.933 --> 45:26.507
but work with that million dollars if the proposed

45:26.555 --> 45:30.321
property tax gets passed. We do

45:30.369 --> 45:34.659
have the ability to do a budget amendment and maybe add in some

45:35.159 --> 45:38.820
additional positions later in the year, but I don't want to confidently say

45:39.463 --> 45:43.158
that that money exists within the budget today, that $4.5 million

45:43.658 --> 45:49.022
or $5 million. My personal mindset

45:49.522 --> 45:53.007
or personal restraint, just from a financial planning standpoint,

45:53.023 --> 45:56.027
I believe Kim taught us this principle of if, come.

45:56.479 --> 46:00.425
I think we should wait until that all gets resolved

46:00.846 --> 46:05.520
and then maybe come back later in the year and discuss adding

46:05.552 --> 46:09.257
additional positions. I had some of the same questions

46:09.757 --> 46:13.897
in my mind. The 3 positions that were being put forward as

46:13.946 --> 46:16.675
P1s were the City Attorney,

46:18.402 --> 46:21.486
the Purchasing Agent, and the Community Service Director.

46:22.497 --> 46:26.588
I, in my initial review, those 3 were removed,

46:26.604 --> 46:30.213
which was about 3, about a third of the cost in those 3

46:30.713 --> 46:34.143
positions. And I felt like maybe we could get more bang for

46:34.643 --> 46:38.218
our buck adding in, um, some of these maybe

46:38.718 --> 46:42.773
lower paid positions but could have a good ROI or return.

46:43.463 --> 46:46.800
So I, in my review, what I did

46:47.300 --> 46:50.668
is I took some of those P2s like the open space

46:51.168 --> 46:55.135
technician, the open space lead, the parks crew lead,

46:55.906 --> 46:59.184
the parks technician that were on

46:59.684 --> 47:03.409
that list and pull those forward to be funded sooner rather than

47:04.389 --> 47:07.298
later to get our parks just in a,

47:07.458 --> 47:11.298
in a, I think, a better operating condition. A good friend

47:11.798 --> 47:15.331
of mine is a coach, and one of the kids, they're playing soccer

47:15.763 --> 47:18.916
And he asked him to kneel down and all the kids were kind of complaining

47:19.416 --> 47:22.906
of how hard the ground was. It just wasn't soft grass.

47:23.083 --> 47:26.529
And I feel like that's an issue that we need to

47:26.546 --> 47:30.284
address in my local park, which I

47:30.397 --> 47:35.309
use as kind of an anecdote. There are 3 tree stumps

47:35.921 --> 47:39.160
that whoever came out, I reported that the trees

47:39.660 --> 47:43.577
were dead. They came out, cut them down. And there's just stumps in our

47:43.657 --> 47:47.297
field, which is a liability to the city. I don't know how many more

47:47.990 --> 47:51.275
of our 60+ parks have that same thing.

47:51.775 --> 47:55.237
I prefer to have somebody come out, take the stump out,

47:55.318 --> 47:58.877
replace it with a tree or sod over it. So I

47:59.377 --> 48:02.987
feel like we are understaffed in that space, and that just shows in our city,

48:03.899 --> 48:06.583
um, across our parks.

48:07.627 --> 48:11.086
So those were some of my thoughts. There was one position

48:11.118 --> 48:15.869
that I would like to advance for the committee to consider, and that's replacing

48:16.369 --> 48:20.185
Todd with an actual biologist. I believe

48:20.685 --> 48:23.896
Council Member Clark and I were discussing this,

48:23.993 --> 48:27.640
and we were looking at that salary range with benefits. I think—

48:28.140 --> 48:31.861
and Council Member Clark can correct me— she was looking at the

48:32.136 --> 48:36.409
cost there, but it was about, I think, $95,000 or something

48:36.425 --> 48:39.918
like that. But I, I feel like a biologist can help us keep

48:40.418 --> 48:43.937
on track with our wildlife corridor and help the developers to

48:44.437 --> 48:47.794
really understand that we're serious about maintaining the

48:48.294 --> 48:51.731
ecosystem as best we can. Although there's developers with

48:52.231 --> 48:55.761
vested rights, we just can't lose or miss

48:56.261 --> 48:57.817
that, that part of the spirit of Eagle Mountain.

48:59.943 --> 49:03.311
Protecting those open spaces, the biologist can help with the wildlife

49:03.327 --> 49:06.790
corridor, can meet with the

49:07.290 --> 49:10.530
developers, but also help us

49:11.030 --> 49:13.170
to manage some of these open spaces that we have.

49:13.670 --> 49:17.090
Todd, they brought out goats last year, and the kids loved having

49:17.590 --> 49:20.810
goats in our neighborhood. Like, 3 or 4 areas, these goats would just,

49:21.310 --> 49:24.830
like, roam around, and it was kind of fun. We've got a lot

49:25.330 --> 49:29.310
of open spaces that need to be considered. Do we sell them to adjacent

49:29.810 --> 49:33.750
homeowners, or do we do something unique with those open

49:34.250 --> 49:38.132
spaces that we have? So I just wanted to advance the biologist

49:38.521 --> 49:41.256
role, which was neither a P1 or a P2,

49:42.568 --> 49:46.300
into this budget. And then maybe just the last

49:46.606 --> 49:51.599
couple other thoughts in terms of parks and

49:52.099 --> 49:55.875
roads. And I don't know if this was in— I can't remember if this

49:56.375 --> 49:59.633
was in the budget, but having an egress for Lone Tree,

49:59.749 --> 50:03.081
we have got to figure that out. That's got to be a

50:03.581 --> 50:07.399
priority, I feel like, to help those residents alleviate some

50:07.899 --> 50:11.310
pressure, putting a road from Lone Tree to Airport Road,

50:11.760 --> 50:15.199
and that'll also help alleviate some of the other traffic that we're seeing on our

50:15.699 --> 50:19.769
road, on our other roads like Ranches Parkway and

50:20.269 --> 50:23.498
Pony Express. I'm supportive of

50:23.998 --> 50:27.738
renewing or rebuilding, or however we're looking at Nolan Park.

50:28.524 --> 50:31.715
That's a park that, uh, we have a splash pad that is

50:32.068 --> 50:35.308
currently nonfunctional, or it's functional but is

50:35.340 --> 50:39.477
just a water waste or something. The 30,000 gallons

50:40.134 --> 50:43.919
a day when the splash pad is turned on. Um, I talked

50:44.419 --> 50:47.623
to the mayor about having some kind of, uh, recycling process,

50:48.329 --> 50:51.456
which that there's going to be some cost there, and maybe adding in some parking

50:51.956 --> 50:55.086
spots, adding in absorbent Observatory at,

50:55.230 --> 50:58.364
uh, Walden Park. I think I'm supportive of

50:58.864 --> 51:02.895
that. And, um, those were just a few of my thoughts.

51:03.040 --> 51:04.235
I'll just pause there.

51:07.363 --> 51:10.377
Mayor, if I could just reply for clarification,

51:10.892 --> 51:14.245
the wildlife biologist position is included in the tentative budget.

51:14.438 --> 51:18.173
It is on that list, um, as a, as a P1.

51:18.435 --> 51:23.181
It just kind of towards the bottom.

51:23.229 --> 51:27.823
And Zach, I would have, have you speak to the road

51:28.323 --> 51:30.499
you're talking about with Lone Tree. We're actually making a lot of progress on that.

51:30.563 --> 51:34.003
So when you say connect to Airport Road, for instance,

51:34.342 --> 51:37.006
one of the things that David and I have been trying to figure out,

51:37.329 --> 51:42.013
and as well as Zach and others, is do

51:42.061 --> 51:44.933
we roto-mill the dirt path?

51:45.062 --> 51:48.820
Or I mean, what is it when you say some improvements? Like right now we've

51:49.320 --> 51:53.470
cut in a It's not great, I don't think, but you can— there's

51:53.970 --> 51:58.056
a dirt path the back way that goes down and connects towards over—

51:58.427 --> 52:01.842
yeah, Ochre Mountain towards Overland. Would an improvement

52:01.987 --> 52:05.578
in your mind be make that dirt path

52:06.077 --> 52:09.084
gravel or roto-mill or to where it's, you know,

52:09.229 --> 52:12.734
fairly usable or what's— what, you know,

52:12.766 --> 52:16.687
I mean, we talking about $10 million road or we talking about

52:16.817 --> 52:17.929
making it better than it is.

52:20.417 --> 52:23.653
Yeah, I think my mind is creating a long-term

52:24.153 --> 52:27.648
solution, egress from Lone Tree to Airport Road,

52:27.696 --> 52:32.079
if that is a possibility, that we start working towards a longer-term

52:32.579 --> 52:35.928
solution to relieve the traffic that's going on

52:35.960 --> 52:39.325
to Pony Express. And I think that— and that's why I was asking,

52:39.357 --> 52:42.936
because all that will be paid for by development. Over time,

52:42.984 --> 52:46.163
and it's actually fairly soon, especially coming from the south?

52:47.062 --> 52:50.498
Or are you saying allocate general fund,

52:51.253 --> 52:54.769
which means tax dollars, to make that happen sooner rather than later at the

52:55.269 --> 52:57.884
benefit of the developer? Could we do it— sorry,

52:58.639 --> 53:02.830
before he answers that question, could we do it sooner by

53:03.039 --> 53:06.667
us finding money and then reimbursing the

53:07.167 --> 53:10.409
fund that the developers would have to pay later because

53:11.163 --> 53:13.652
it's in the camp.

53:14.872 --> 53:18.148
So once we build it, it's theirs. They don't pay us back.

53:18.710 --> 53:22.563
And that's why it's been— and we have made deals with Ivory

53:22.644 --> 53:26.016
to specifically speed

53:26.516 --> 53:29.949
that one up. And they said they would just as a favor. They're going to

53:30.449 --> 53:34.059
build clear to their border. So the only— we're just missing a small section

53:34.123 --> 53:37.781
there that would be— I think that's on John Hatfield's property

53:38.054 --> 53:41.422
possibly, and it would connect. But I don't know that he's

53:41.922 --> 53:46.074
in any hurry. So, you know, and that's why I said it would— a gravel

53:46.574 --> 53:49.668
or roto-mill— roto-mill packs down. I mean, Lake Mountain Road,

53:49.684 --> 53:53.662
everybody on that road's driving on gravel. I mean, we could make it drivable

53:54.336 --> 53:58.041
without paving it. I'm asking for direction

53:58.541 --> 54:02.000
of when you advocate for that, what, what What is it that you

54:02.116 --> 54:04.121
expect to see and put a dollar amount to it?

54:07.112 --> 54:10.559
And you're welcome to— can I step in here a little bit, Mayor? I mean,

54:10.575 --> 54:14.327
I think number one, we should recognize that Ivory did agree to advance the timetable,

54:14.697 --> 54:18.017
and I'm grateful that they're willing to do that coming from the south, because what

54:18.049 --> 54:21.563
we're really trying to do is just create some emergency ingress and egress

54:21.611 --> 54:25.474
and a permanent solution at some point in time. I think

54:25.539 --> 54:28.882
we've got to, at a minimum, I think we've got to work with those those

54:29.382 --> 54:33.179
landowners and work some sort of, even if it's just an easement

54:33.211 --> 54:36.465
with the right to put down some sort of a road base that

54:36.481 --> 54:39.864
will act as a, as a, as a beginning point

54:40.364 --> 54:44.048
for a road at the city's expense. I would be okay with that.

54:44.112 --> 54:46.741
What I don't want to do is I don't want to take $10 million or

54:47.241 --> 54:50.605
$15 million out of the city's budget to build that road when the developers are

54:51.105 --> 54:53.747
going to come build it anyway. They're just not doing it as quickly as we'd

54:54.247 --> 54:57.070
like them to, but I know that there's some discussion and some work that's happening

54:57.102 --> 55:00.795
there. I just want to create a meaningful egress for the folks

55:01.295 --> 55:03.107
in Lone Tree, and I think that's what Zach is saying. I think that's what

55:03.607 --> 55:08.037
every council member here has felt. We just want to create some meaningful ingress

55:08.101 --> 55:11.954
and egress for those folks. So, so to be fair, and I

55:12.454 --> 55:15.551
want to make sure that we're giving clear direction, so Zach might have to speak

55:16.051 --> 55:19.646
to this if you want to move forward. I'm talking to Hilton, not, not councilman

55:20.146 --> 55:24.214
He was— but I talked to Zach a few

55:24.714 --> 55:28.062
weeks ago about some roto-mill projects. Maybe you could speak to

55:28.562 --> 55:32.294
it. I mean, I know we're throwing darts, but give us some ideas on dollar

55:32.326 --> 55:35.228
amounts and what it would take to make that road, you know,

55:36.639 --> 55:40.182
minivan accessible. Not— do we know if Perry

55:40.682 --> 55:44.237
or if Hatfield are willing? And does the road split their property lines,

55:44.270 --> 55:47.814
or is it on one? Me, David, too. So there's

55:48.314 --> 55:51.334
two, maybe two comments here. Turn the mic on.

55:54.594 --> 55:58.656
There we go. Uh, as, as far as maintaining what's

55:58.802 --> 56:02.897
existing there, um, we currently have

56:03.026 --> 56:06.538
the Eagle Mountain Boulevard mill and overlay,

56:06.554 --> 56:10.340
which is tentatively planned

56:10.840 --> 56:14.077
to be done like next week. We are planning on keeping those millings.

56:14.869 --> 56:19.802
And so we do have a product, right, that it

56:20.302 --> 56:23.522
won't— there's going to be some cost, but it's all

56:24.022 --> 56:27.419
in-house. It wouldn't be anything that would cost us extra that we've talked about adding

56:27.467 --> 56:30.507
to it, at least

56:31.025 --> 56:34.630
at the entrance sides, mostly for dust control. The existing road that

56:35.130 --> 56:39.036
we have there now, we've drove it throughout

56:39.069 --> 56:44.203
the winter and multiple times of the year where

56:44.251 --> 56:48.038
a car could easily access it. I would say the

56:48.538 --> 56:51.617
primary concern would be where it's entering either side of the subdivision, that we have

56:52.117 --> 56:55.764
some form of dust control. Whether or not the millions would be enough to

56:55.780 --> 56:58.881
cover that whole road or not, it would certainly at least be enough to do

56:59.511 --> 57:03.145
either end. And it would be stuff that we're prepared to do

57:03.645 --> 57:06.925
here shortly. And real quick before David jumps on there,

57:07.249 --> 57:11.361
Council Member Hewisch, do you— I take for granted

57:11.861 --> 57:14.214
that people might know when we say we put roto-mill down what that means.

57:14.280 --> 57:15.899
Does everybody understand what that does?

57:17.973 --> 57:21.476
Explain it? Okay, so what they do is they're basically grinding

57:21.508 --> 57:24.963
down 2 inches, 1.5 inches of asphalt over

57:25.463 --> 57:28.894
on a different road. We're doing 3 inches of replacement. And it's for the full

57:29.026 --> 57:32.710
length of the median. By— Yes.

57:33.127 --> 57:35.954
So it's asphalt shavings, and it's— you mentioned road base.

57:36.307 --> 57:39.214
It's like— it comes out about the size of road base. They can put it

57:39.714 --> 57:41.960
down and roll it, and as the sun gets on that— a lot of my

57:42.089 --> 57:45.397
driveway is actually roto-milled tailings. The sun gets

57:45.897 --> 57:48.432
on there, there's so much oil content in there that it actually kind of compacts,

57:48.850 --> 57:53.234
and you wouldn't know that it wasn't paved with an asphalt machine other

57:53.330 --> 57:56.767
than you can see the little chunks. So that— by throwing down

57:56.943 --> 58:00.468
asphalt tailings that we already have. The sun gets

58:00.564 --> 58:04.444
on that, they level it out, it becomes almost

58:04.944 --> 58:08.373
like a road. Now I think in order to keep the integrity there, and we'd

58:08.873 --> 58:11.498
have an engineer speak to this, we might want to throw down a skiff of

58:11.530 --> 58:14.736
road base so that we don't have it— I don't know what the ground looks

58:15.236 --> 58:18.857
like under it, or does it have road base? It does not, but it

58:19.357 --> 58:23.317
is very stable, right? We basically just graded out an old

58:23.817 --> 58:27.220
road that's been there for who knows how long, forever.

58:27.525 --> 58:31.539
If it's stable and you feel like the tailings are gonna— Yeah,

58:31.683 --> 58:35.039
I think the benefit of the tailings would be both dust control as

58:35.232 --> 58:38.539
well as just helping muddy, right?

58:38.604 --> 58:42.409
Like it prevents your car from— if it's raining or anything else, you don't get

58:42.522 --> 58:45.717
ruts and muddy. I want to make sure we're talking about the same thing,

58:45.749 --> 58:49.330
because I think what I'm hearing you say, Zach, is improve the temporary

58:49.830 --> 58:54.009
temporary egress that exists today, right? Correct. Yes. And I'm not

58:54.509 --> 58:57.775
sure that that's what I'm hearing from council. So I want

58:57.951 --> 59:00.867
to, I want to kind of back up a little bit. What I'm hearing is,

59:01.300 --> 59:05.322
at least what I'm hoping, is that we can find some forward momentum on

59:05.370 --> 59:09.312
that permanent egress and, and ingress into

59:09.377 --> 59:12.630
Lone Tree. And what I mean by that is work with the property owners to,

59:12.982 --> 59:16.604
to find the road, to design it to locate it,

59:17.101 --> 59:20.691
to secure property either through an easement or a future

59:20.755 --> 59:24.378
purchase contract. And then if we want to do some of

59:24.878 --> 59:28.305
what you're talking about on that, I see that. One of the complaints that we

59:28.805 --> 59:32.231
get all the time is that that just spins them around and puts them out

59:32.731 --> 59:36.030
100 feet from their egress right now onto

59:36.078 --> 59:40.181
the same highway. And if we improve that,

59:40.486 --> 59:45.503
it makes no meaningful difference or very little meaningful difference to the residents

59:46.003 --> 59:46.739
who live in that subdivision. So I want to be clear that we're talking about

59:47.239 --> 59:50.091
two different things here. Connecting— I thought that's what it was too, so thanks for

59:50.591 --> 59:54.807
the clarity. So we're talking about connecting to the south on— South to Overland.

59:55.465 --> 59:58.769
Well, I just want to add one quick thing to

59:59.269 --> 01:00:03.003
that too. Like, if we're talking about the actual future alignment of a

01:00:03.503 --> 01:00:07.190
roadway, so we're kind of pre-building the road, right? We have to take

01:00:07.690 --> 01:00:10.831
in consideration we still have water, sewer, we have all the, all the things that

01:00:10.863 --> 01:00:14.405
go underneath the road before we build the road. Well, those are the things we

01:00:14.905 --> 01:00:17.546
need to know about. And, and, and, and, and if you can help us understand

01:00:18.046 --> 01:00:20.463
that the only way we can do this is it's going to be X,

01:00:20.559 --> 01:00:23.587
Y, or Z, this is the only meaningful way that we should tackle that.

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:26.584
I just don't feel like we have those answers yet.

01:00:27.049 --> 01:00:30.222
Yeah. So the section of road we're talking about, it's a little under a mile.

01:00:30.670 --> 01:00:33.971
It's like 5,100 feet to, to the end of

01:00:34.471 --> 01:00:39.836
Arnett Peak. So Overland's doing Arnett Peak, which comes up north of Airport

01:00:40.336 --> 01:00:41.087
road. So you have Fiona down here and you have Arnett Peak. It goes up

01:00:41.167 --> 01:00:44.277
and turns and stops at their property line. Be, let's say,

01:00:44.293 --> 01:00:47.723
1,500 feet up and over to Lone Tree Parkway. Pricing it

01:00:48.223 --> 01:00:51.025
out, the road was about $5 million if we just were to build the road,

01:00:51.041 --> 01:00:54.664
and it would be like a ribbon road, 26 feet width of pavement,

01:00:55.257 --> 01:00:58.318
um, but that would not include utilities. We'd have to at least put

01:00:58.818 --> 01:01:02.389
water in there as well to— for the future developments. And so

01:01:02.870 --> 01:01:05.996
you asked about alignment. We tried looking at placing perfect

01:01:06.496 --> 01:01:10.036
connection where the road would be straight and over. That kind of chopped up JDH's

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:13.562
property, um, preliminary layouts, so they didn't like that.

01:01:13.643 --> 01:01:17.041
So it'll probably have to go on the border, kind of spanning,

01:01:17.394 --> 01:01:20.744
uh, Perry and then JDH. Um, so that does make the collector kind

01:01:21.244 --> 01:01:24.832
of jog a little bit. It's not awful, we've done it before. Um, but yeah,

01:01:24.928 --> 01:01:27.653
the concern is just the funding of it. Like, there are a lot of other

01:01:28.153 --> 01:01:30.427
problems— projects in the city that we could use that money for other things.

01:01:31.164 --> 01:01:34.840
I know it's important to So can we use the mill tailings

01:01:34.952 --> 01:01:39.047
to connect to Arnett? I think that's the question. And if

01:01:39.547 --> 01:01:42.595
that's not the case, then do we save those mill tailings for some other

01:01:43.061 --> 01:01:46.786
meaningful place and figure out the issue that we've all

01:01:46.834 --> 01:01:50.046
been trying to address, right? But— So I

01:01:50.592 --> 01:01:54.268
think part of the issue is now multiple summers,

01:01:54.317 --> 01:01:57.720
because this is your 5th summer going into— serving

01:01:57.752 --> 01:02:01.920
on council, this has come up. It's come up

01:02:02.420 --> 01:02:06.024
for a while in the last couple of years, and every single year it's like,

01:02:06.088 --> 01:02:08.974
yeah, we're working on it, we're working with the landowners, you know,

01:02:09.439 --> 01:02:12.228
like all those things. And this isn't a slam on any staff member or anything.

01:02:12.725 --> 01:02:16.413
I get that this is a Herculean task, but when we sit in

01:02:16.913 --> 01:02:20.228
the neighborhood— and trust me, Councilmember Wright and I have

01:02:20.260 --> 01:02:23.949
sat in this neighborhood and really been pelted feeling frustrated by the people

01:02:24.449 --> 01:02:27.947
who live there, and rightly so, that we haven't addressed this problem.

01:02:28.525 --> 01:02:32.635
And I think that if they could

01:02:32.716 --> 01:02:36.521
see that in 18

01:02:37.021 --> 01:02:40.262
months, this is what's going to happen, in 3 years, this is what's going to

01:02:40.326 --> 01:02:43.361
happen, the temporary fix is

01:02:43.474 --> 01:02:46.974
this, and we have those things moving forward

01:02:47.474 --> 01:02:52.254
with meaningful progress, I think the people of Lone Tree would be way

01:02:52.462 --> 01:02:56.248
more secure and believing what

01:02:56.297 --> 01:02:59.425
we say we're planning on doing. I think that

01:02:59.925 --> 01:03:03.340
this area is a classic example of it's either too

01:03:03.372 --> 01:03:07.110
expensive or too complicated, so it just kind of keeps getting bumped down

01:03:07.142 --> 01:03:10.271
on the list of priorities, even though it's super important,

01:03:10.415 --> 01:03:13.560
because there are a ton of people that live there, and we saw

01:03:13.720 --> 01:03:17.472
what happened fluke occurrence a

01:03:17.972 --> 01:03:22.448
couple summers ago when one of their entrances was blocked and

01:03:22.948 --> 01:03:26.725
there was a fire truck that was like— it was just the perfect storm of

01:03:26.824 --> 01:03:29.185
problems. And then even last fall,

01:03:30.870 --> 01:03:34.591
two contractors showed up at the exact same time and shut down both

01:03:35.091 --> 01:03:38.555
entrances of that neighborhood. And you've done a great job of helping us address some

01:03:39.055 --> 01:03:43.211
of those things with code, But if we can't say that

01:03:43.421 --> 01:03:46.982
we are going to do something at this point and this point and this point,

01:03:47.224 --> 01:03:51.085
then we haven't solved the problem. And I think we

01:03:51.425 --> 01:03:54.644
need to find a way to solve it. But I think the question— I mean,

01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:57.620
I don't— I'm going to put it this way. I think the problem lies with

01:03:58.120 --> 01:04:00.050
the council. I'm just going to put it back on our plate.

01:04:02.151 --> 01:04:04.826
Because what I've heard David say is we can build the road for $5 million.

01:04:07.490 --> 01:04:11.241
It's really a question of, are we willing to take the citizens' resources that

01:04:11.741 --> 01:04:15.007
come from all around the city, their tax dollars, and build that road?

01:04:16.069 --> 01:04:20.213
Or do we just kind of keep waiting until development builds

01:04:20.713 --> 01:04:23.596
that road and it doesn't have to come out of the citizens' resources? That's the

01:04:24.096 --> 01:04:27.929
quandary that I'm in, and I'm not certain

01:04:28.429 --> 01:04:31.717
how to answer that, to be completely honest with you, because I've wrestled with this.

01:04:33.194 --> 01:04:35.827
And that's part of why I'm trying to find a balance. If there's some way

01:04:36.327 --> 01:04:39.681
that we can secure the right-of-way. One thing we

01:04:40.181 --> 01:04:43.390
could do, we could do the proposed solution this summer. You know,

01:04:43.775 --> 01:04:46.826
kind of put the tailings down for that temporary access now, as far as the

01:04:47.326 --> 01:04:50.712
immediate solution. We could start engaging with them to look at some kind of easement

01:04:51.081 --> 01:04:54.469
and maybe do some kind of on-site improvements, maybe blade it, grade it,

01:04:54.614 --> 01:04:57.184
put some more millions down in, you know, the the next, I don't know,

01:04:57.200 --> 01:05:01.450
6 months to a year, however long that would take to create that temporary access

01:05:01.950 --> 01:05:04.510
in the meantime. We could just engage them with that. Those are the kinds of

01:05:05.010 --> 01:05:08.431
solutions, David, that I would like to examine because to be completely

01:05:08.705 --> 01:05:12.505
candid, I'm uncomfortable spending $5 million of

01:05:13.005 --> 01:05:13.685
taxpayer resources.

01:05:18.208 --> 01:05:22.594
There are a lot of needs in the city, and when you look at— $5

01:05:23.094 --> 01:05:24.870
million goes a long way. Goes a long way in the city, right? And so

01:05:25.370 --> 01:05:30.563
to dedicate that to one road project that is— that's

01:05:30.611 --> 01:05:34.058
a real problem, but that has been created through decades

01:05:34.074 --> 01:05:38.137
of development and an unwillingness

01:05:38.637 --> 01:05:42.182
or an inability for us to really create some connectivity there. So,

01:05:42.199 --> 01:05:45.474
Brad, you got something to say, so step up to the plate, my friend.

01:05:45.963 --> 01:05:50.194
I've just been involved in this process for the last few years. And so we

01:05:50.694 --> 01:05:54.985
have met with those landowners in that area. And I think, I think

01:05:55.485 --> 01:05:59.506
we can work with them. I think we can figure out easements.

01:06:00.575 --> 01:06:03.491
But I think that we're going to have to kind of work with their conceptual

01:06:03.991 --> 01:06:08.496
plans and what they have as well, because this minor

01:06:08.996 --> 01:06:13.007
collector that we're proposing through there, or this collector

01:06:13.507 --> 01:06:16.729
road that we're proposing through there, The alignment of it

01:06:17.229 --> 01:06:20.599
today would take away to where they couldn't have lots that front

01:06:21.099 --> 01:06:24.565
onto it. So then they're going to lose some of their buildable lots.

01:06:25.175 --> 01:06:28.948
And so there's technicalities of it. And so I think that we

01:06:29.448 --> 01:06:31.919
can have those discussions with the developer, but I think that there's going to be

01:06:32.419 --> 01:06:35.483
some asks on the table that we're going to need to encounter as well,

01:06:35.788 --> 01:06:39.449
to be truthful and honest. I think that that's what we've seen

01:06:39.949 --> 01:06:43.876
as we've gone through this process. We did propose early on that we wanted

01:06:44.376 --> 01:06:47.594
to see it right on the lot line with the Perry land and all

01:06:47.626 --> 01:06:50.911
of that, because then it's split and

01:06:51.411 --> 01:06:54.789
it's on the edge of the property and it doesn't dissect it. But there,

01:06:55.318 --> 01:06:58.747
I think we can go negotiate those things and we can

01:06:58.763 --> 01:07:01.792
pursue some of those things to see if we can get some easements. But I

01:07:01.824 --> 01:07:04.372
think there's going to have to be some formal process that's going to have to

01:07:04.872 --> 01:07:08.716
take place in that with that. Well, then Then I don't— I'm one member of

01:07:09.216 --> 01:07:11.506
5 that's going to get to vote on this, but my encouragement would be let's

01:07:12.006 --> 01:07:15.130
go do it. Let's have those conversations sooner rather than later. I know that they're

01:07:15.630 --> 01:07:18.931
preliminary and that we've had them, but let's go firm something up. And if it

01:07:18.963 --> 01:07:22.299
means that we need to look at some conceptual plans or enter into an

01:07:22.331 --> 01:07:25.763
MDA that gives them certain rights to allow us to

01:07:25.779 --> 01:07:29.355
create some connectivity through this area, I'm willing to have a conversation

01:07:29.387 --> 01:07:32.706
about that. I don't know if the council is, but I am.

01:07:33.888 --> 01:07:37.091
I've got some very strong opinion about what I'm about to say here.

01:07:37.847 --> 01:07:41.548
The land immediately west of Lone Tree has

01:07:41.709 --> 01:07:44.271
no right for residential use.

01:07:45.657 --> 01:07:48.688
It is agricultural zone until this body says otherwise.

01:07:49.272 --> 01:07:53.770
And the reason why I bring that up is I— when

01:07:53.802 --> 01:07:57.750
I hear that developers are creating plans that do not align with

01:07:58.250 --> 01:08:01.410
the zone Or the future land use map. Maybe I need to take the

01:08:01.910 --> 01:08:04.690
future land use map into consideration here. But even that, the future land use map

01:08:05.190 --> 01:08:08.570
is not the gospel. The zone is. And they haven't talked

01:08:09.070 --> 01:08:11.470
to the city council yet. I haven't been approached. I don't think any of you

01:08:11.970 --> 01:08:14.430
have been approached about plans west of Lone Tree. I'd like to see what those

01:08:14.930 --> 01:08:17.170
plans are, what they're wanting to do, and that they come and talk to us

01:08:17.670 --> 01:08:21.130
first. I have seen some residential concept. Okay. And I

01:08:21.630 --> 01:08:25.578
hadn't heard anything yet. But for me, if a developer wants to change a

01:08:26.078 --> 01:08:29.410
zone, they really need to come talk to us first and get us involved

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:32.938
early instead of, you know, what I'm hearing, well, the road's not going to align

01:08:33.438 --> 01:08:37.172
well. Well, in my book, they can farm the land, and I'm not being— I'm

01:08:37.188 --> 01:08:40.796
not saying this in a mean way or whatever. I'm just saying that's what

01:08:41.296 --> 01:08:46.152
the land use is currently. The zone is agricultural. And so— and

01:08:46.652 --> 01:08:49.647
this is what— this is the only tool that allows the city to try to

01:08:49.679 --> 01:08:53.850
plan a little bit larger have a larger scope with

01:08:53.866 --> 01:08:57.222
which we can plan the city and connect different

01:08:57.722 --> 01:09:00.754
areas, because if they want to change it from agricultural to residential,

01:09:00.786 --> 01:09:03.900
then we can get some— then we can say yes, but we need to align

01:09:04.400 --> 01:09:06.790
the road in a way that will work for us as well. And I know,

01:09:07.031 --> 01:09:09.953
yes, we want to be collaborative. I think the city council here has shown that

01:09:09.985 --> 01:09:13.067
we are collaborative with developers. We're not punitive.

01:09:13.597 --> 01:09:16.824
But I— anyway, when I hear that they're

01:09:17.324 --> 01:09:20.060
planning and I haven't seen it, I want to be in on what's going on

01:09:20.560 --> 01:09:24.107
with, with plans. Okay, now I want to go to

01:09:24.187 --> 01:09:27.304
Council Member Hewish real quick and get a comment.

01:09:30.853 --> 01:09:34.057
Good second. All right, the, uh, I guess you

01:09:34.557 --> 01:09:38.147
can hear me. The audio is not great, so what I'm going to assume is

01:09:38.324 --> 01:09:41.326
everyone just agreed with everything I had to say.

01:09:42.218 --> 01:09:45.297
That's, that's going to be my assumption,

01:09:45.443 --> 01:09:49.180
and I apologize for not being there on purpose. I'm making this difficult for

01:09:49.680 --> 01:09:52.584
being out of town. Uh, just a few things that,

01:09:53.113 --> 01:09:56.678
um, have really helped me in my personal practice,

01:09:58.027 --> 01:10:01.880
and that is when, when we're, when we're short-staffed,

01:10:02.346 --> 01:10:05.734
oftentimes there's a technology that can help us to

01:10:06.234 --> 01:10:10.037
fill in some of those gaps where sometimes we don't need to hire a person.

01:10:10.374 --> 01:10:14.292
Uh, one of these, and I don't know if These

01:10:14.324 --> 01:10:17.808
tools are currently being utilized, but I would fully support

01:10:18.322 --> 01:10:21.566
having some licenses through Anthropic.

01:10:22.031 --> 01:10:25.901
It doesn't have to be Anthropic, but it is a very high-quality product.

01:10:26.431 --> 01:10:29.979
I think our city attorney should have a license in

01:10:30.479 --> 01:10:33.656
a closed system where private confidential

01:10:34.156 --> 01:10:37.446
items can be analyzed and be a tool

01:10:37.946 --> 01:10:41.539
or resource for him. I would love that as a council member to

01:10:42.039 --> 01:10:46.449
have access to something where we can safely

01:10:47.208 --> 01:10:50.672
take confidential items uploaded into

01:10:51.541 --> 01:10:55.390
Anthropic's products called Claude and evaluate some of these

01:10:56.211 --> 01:10:59.914
NDAs that were expected. I'm not a real estate—

01:11:00.414 --> 01:11:04.502
that's not my background, nor am I an attorney. But I think

01:11:05.002 --> 01:11:08.327
our citizens have an expectation that we do the highest quality job that we can.

01:11:08.599 --> 01:11:12.190
So one, one thing that I would add somewhere here in our budget is that

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:16.054
we have some level of AI technology available

01:11:16.118 --> 01:11:18.859
for staff, available for the council, the mayor,

01:11:19.629 --> 01:11:23.027
uh, for, uh, our, our city manager

01:11:24.117 --> 01:11:28.221
to maybe enhance their or, or extend

01:11:28.721 --> 01:11:31.844
their skill set using, using AI.

01:11:31.973 --> 01:11:36.475
That, that's one ask. I also wonder if we could review the Lifesize

01:11:36.507 --> 01:11:39.880
product that we're currently using. Is there a better product so that we can have

01:11:40.221 --> 01:11:43.869
better connectivity when we're remote?

01:11:44.192 --> 01:11:47.108
And then the last thing I'll just add in there, and I know the mayor

01:11:47.172 --> 01:11:51.431
knows I'm passionate about this, but having a Verizon tower close

01:11:51.931 --> 01:11:55.374
to the city building would probably help half of our employees.

01:11:57.360 --> 01:12:01.248
I'll just— I'll stop there. I'll jump back on to Lifesize

01:12:01.346 --> 01:12:03.535
for the audio. Thank you.

01:12:04.906 --> 01:12:08.263
Okay, so if I can wrap this up, and then I know Councilmember

01:12:08.763 --> 01:12:11.555
Clark wants to— not— sorry, not wrap it up, I mean try to bring this

01:12:11.684 --> 01:12:15.106
together. We don't want to

01:12:15.606 --> 01:12:18.746
use those tailings on the emergency access, but we do want to use them on

01:12:19.246 --> 01:12:22.913
Arnett and try to figure out, um, and I'm

01:12:23.413 --> 01:12:26.957
talking north of Ivory, so the section of Arnett that would connect

01:12:27.457 --> 01:12:31.601
Lone Tree through probably JDH to

01:12:31.682 --> 01:12:35.447
Arnett. And we would rather see that progress,

01:12:35.463 --> 01:12:39.262
which would then also mean stable ground because

01:12:39.762 --> 01:12:41.597
it hasn't been a road forever. Do we need to put a little bit of

01:12:42.097 --> 01:12:46.991
road base under it or what, as opposed to a multimillion-dollar project?

01:12:47.039 --> 01:12:50.275
So is that— I know you have a comment, maybe it's

01:12:50.775 --> 01:12:54.134
on that same thing, but have I— am I clear on that understanding?

01:12:54.414 --> 01:12:58.000
I'm not against using some of those tailings for the existing road if that's the

01:12:58.500 --> 01:13:01.200
best solution that we can come up with. I think it'd be the quickest solution

01:13:01.700 --> 01:13:03.940
for now, and then we could also look at maybe even some more millions later

01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:06.980
on when the easements and alignments are figured out. We can bring that back to

01:13:07.480 --> 01:13:10.840
council. So I think, I think it's kind of staged or phased approach. Maybe that's

01:13:11.340 --> 01:13:15.820
phase 1. Phase 2 is we, we work on the, on the right-of-ways and connections,

01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:19.480
landowners. Let's get it going south sooner rather than

01:13:19.980 --> 01:13:23.071
later. So add on to that, we do an

01:13:23.119 --> 01:13:27.697
unauthorized listening tour, and I know that

01:13:27.729 --> 01:13:30.637
David Yuleberry said that we need to go to Lone Tree to talk to them

01:13:30.701 --> 01:13:34.203
about their egress and their emergency plan and all that kind of good stuff.

01:13:34.846 --> 01:13:38.284
I think it would be useful to make that probably

01:13:38.380 --> 01:13:41.770
our next stop on the listening tour so that we

01:13:42.270 --> 01:13:45.673
can talk to them about it and have a realistic timeline of

01:13:46.011 --> 01:13:49.320
here's the tailings, this is what we're doing, because last year we had a temporary

01:13:49.820 --> 01:13:53.270
solution of another temporary, a little bit better temporary solution this year.

01:13:53.816 --> 01:13:57.734
But to come up with this is kind of like

01:13:58.167 --> 01:14:01.925
a deadline for ourselves to work with the landowners to

01:14:02.425 --> 01:14:05.907
get it done, because I feel like what we've told them is the same

01:14:06.019 --> 01:14:09.808
thing every time. It's very complicated. We're working on the land.

01:14:09.857 --> 01:14:13.823
We're doing all of this stuff, but there's no meaningful evidence

01:14:14.047 --> 01:14:16.901
of that moving forward. And the last thing that I want to say about Lone

01:14:17.014 --> 01:14:20.975
Tree, and I know that we discussed this before and it was kind of put

01:14:21.475 --> 01:14:25.034
on the back burner, but if we can't get all the

01:14:25.534 --> 01:14:28.931
landowners on board to go south, then maybe we need

01:14:29.401 --> 01:14:34.229
to push more with going to the north.

01:14:35.642 --> 01:14:39.459
And I know that that will create a little bit of throughway

01:14:39.959 --> 01:14:43.367
through Lone Tree. But something for Lone Tree is better than

01:14:43.867 --> 01:14:47.012
nothing for Lone Tree. So I know the airport is,

01:14:47.108 --> 01:14:51.620
but there's also the wash and everything else. But we—

01:14:52.503 --> 01:14:55.891
if we can't get it to go south and actually work, then I think we

01:14:56.391 --> 01:14:59.664
need to open up looking at the north again if we have to.

01:14:59.793 --> 01:15:03.582
So, all right, I think it'll work.

01:15:03.646 --> 01:15:06.344
The problem is the funding, and the funding's not gonna be any better to the

01:15:07.172 --> 01:15:10.451
north. So I wrote it down. The issue with funding, Mayor,

01:15:10.531 --> 01:15:13.955
I think has more to do with the fact of how many people does this

01:15:13.987 --> 01:15:18.015
road serve. If the road were something

01:15:18.515 --> 01:15:21.795
that more people could be using or would provide a bigger benefit if it was

