01:31:19.292 --> 01:31:22.745
when I first started as a city manager here in Eagle Mountain, I showed a

01:31:22.970 --> 01:31:26.005
graph where a really good city is a well-balanced city where

01:31:26.022 --> 01:31:29.153
you build a foundation of public safety, quality of life,

01:31:29.169 --> 01:31:32.349
economic development and prosperity, and essential services.

01:31:32.847 --> 01:31:36.910
Right now, arguably, we have put all of our— not all,

01:31:37.039 --> 01:31:40.058
but a significant amount of our resources into public safety,

01:31:40.556 --> 01:31:44.025
which has made it so it's more limiting to some some of the parks improvements

01:31:44.525 --> 01:31:48.408
and open space improvements and other type of things. If the tax increase goes forward

01:31:48.585 --> 01:31:52.069
and we do tie our most stable revenue source to our most needed

01:31:52.776 --> 01:31:56.983
essential service, which is public safety, that does allow this

01:31:57.483 --> 01:32:01.671
council, a future council, to determine how to use those other resources to

01:32:02.073 --> 01:32:06.231
invest in quality of life, economic prosperity, essential services,

01:32:07.147 --> 01:32:10.453
so that you have a more balanced city going forward, but that's up to you

01:32:10.598 --> 01:32:14.386
in the future. I think that you bring up a really good point because we

01:32:14.514 --> 01:32:18.591
are looking at a potential tax increase so that we can

01:32:19.104 --> 01:32:23.117
more adequately fund public safety, because for

01:32:23.617 --> 01:32:27.627
a while our public safety costs have been subsidized by other parts

01:32:28.127 --> 01:32:30.773
of the budget. And I think it's really important for our residents to hear that

01:32:30.885 --> 01:32:34.079
when we talk about a tax increase, it's not for the things that are on

01:32:34.095 --> 01:32:37.211
this paper, but without a tax increase,

01:32:37.886 --> 01:32:41.870
we're going to have to continue subsidizing

01:32:41.886 --> 01:32:45.645
the public safety part, the cost of our budget, with other

01:32:45.757 --> 01:32:49.307
areas of revenue. So I think without

01:32:49.532 --> 01:32:52.873
that tax increase to fund public safety,

01:32:53.451 --> 01:32:57.082
these things that are on this paper are not

01:32:57.582 --> 01:33:00.857
possible, correct? And I— You're absolutely correct. That's what I just want to make

01:33:01.357 --> 01:33:05.404
sure as we're having this conversation And the other piece too is like us cutting

01:33:05.500 --> 01:33:08.955
something versus tentative budget. When the tentative

01:33:08.987 --> 01:33:12.733
budget was brought to us, it was very clearly said that this

01:33:13.233 --> 01:33:16.661
is where staff and the mayor hand the budget over to council

01:33:17.015 --> 01:33:20.879
to look at and pursue. It's not like this is a given and

01:33:21.379 --> 01:33:24.605
this is all said and done. So I think that's the way we approached

01:33:25.105 --> 01:33:27.135
it. So as long as we're all on the same page, we're good.

01:33:28.056 --> 01:33:31.407
Yes, sir. None of this is possible if we don't do something.

01:33:31.615 --> 01:33:34.052
That's why I'm trying to determine what that is.

01:33:34.966 --> 01:33:39.294
Not going through every piece, is there anything outside of the 5

01:33:39.794 --> 01:33:43.222
that were just listed? Are all the other ones on the list

01:33:44.023 --> 01:33:47.309
proposal okay to say that we're good to advance?

01:33:47.406 --> 01:33:50.403
Could I get somewhat of a consensus that we're okay with all the rest of

01:33:50.903 --> 01:33:54.283
the positions? Other than the 5. I have

01:33:54.783 --> 01:33:58.195
one question before I answer that, Mayor, and it really goes back to what Councilmember

01:33:58.695 --> 01:34:01.978
Hewish said. As you look at the potential funding source, those that are proposed

01:34:02.042 --> 01:34:05.328
to be funded with sales tax/rap tax,

01:34:06.547 --> 01:34:09.160
I tend to agree with the statement that he made about the rap tax,

01:34:09.176 --> 01:34:13.344
that I don't think we should be dedicating rap tax to ongoing— I think,

01:34:13.424 --> 01:34:16.839
I think I actually agree with that, and my understanding is there,

01:34:17.031 --> 01:34:20.303
there was some column that we had talked about where the potential,

01:34:20.576 --> 01:34:24.754
but at this point, all of these could be funded through the general fund except

01:34:25.254 --> 01:34:28.546
for— Well, I think the way that Kimberly's broken this down is really helpful.

01:34:28.610 --> 01:34:32.611
The transportation tax, permit fees, development fees, utility fees, all those are funded

01:34:33.111 --> 01:34:36.709
from different sources. The upper section is the only one that, you know,

01:34:36.789 --> 01:34:40.854
down through, through what is it, the recreation and sports coordinator,

01:34:42.268 --> 01:34:45.530
that's from there up are the only ones that are impacted

01:34:46.030 --> 01:34:48.533
by sales tax. Everything else is funded from different funding mechanisms. So,

01:34:49.560 --> 01:34:53.366
I mean, that for me automatically makes those on the lower portion of

01:34:53.414 --> 01:34:56.786
the list easier to deal with, because they're funded from other sources

01:34:57.286 --> 01:35:01.025
where those revenues are strong and where they're able to be pledged and

01:35:01.057 --> 01:35:04.284
taken. I think loud and clear that those are

01:35:04.332 --> 01:35:07.897
possible. They qualify, like the open space qualifies for ABTAX,

01:35:07.945 --> 01:35:10.691
but I don't know that— I think I agree with you that I would rather

01:35:11.191 --> 01:35:12.881
not I don't see it funded from there. So the question is,

01:35:14.653 --> 01:35:18.236
when we hear that this is funded in the tentative budget,

01:35:18.253 --> 01:35:21.601
the question that I have is, is it contemplated by being paid

01:35:22.101 --> 01:35:25.048
with RAP tax, or have we calculated it to come out of sales tax proceeds?

01:35:25.144 --> 01:35:28.768
It is currently calculated with sales tax. So we

01:35:29.268 --> 01:35:32.680
did not allocate any RAP tax whatsoever towards anything.

01:35:34.519 --> 01:35:37.977
I just was trying to identify that that would would be a potential source if

01:35:38.477 --> 01:35:42.058
you needed it. Okay. That's completely up to the body

01:35:42.558 --> 01:35:45.126
if they want to use those funds or potential funds that could be used.

01:35:45.206 --> 01:35:48.901
But right now they're all coming— they're all proposed to be general fund

01:35:49.401 --> 01:35:53.078
expenses. That's what I— so then are we

01:35:53.578 --> 01:35:57.368
good moving forward with— I know now I'm going to be sensitive

01:35:57.868 --> 01:36:01.095
because I know Councilmember Hewish maybe said not some of

01:36:01.595 --> 01:36:04.457
the other P2s, but then he put back in the open space ones. Which really

01:36:04.489 --> 01:36:08.286
only means 2 of the recreation and 2 of the building and the wastewater,

01:36:08.351 --> 01:36:11.744
if I'm not mistaken. So I did hear that, and I don't

01:36:12.244 --> 01:36:14.759
know if he's still able to comment, but I want to know, other than the

01:36:15.259 --> 01:36:18.728
5 positions, is everybody else willing to advance the entire list?

01:36:20.930 --> 01:36:24.217
Yes. So, yeah, I was going to say, which one is

01:36:24.717 --> 01:36:28.536
5? The ones that Ben just mentioned is the attorney, the purchasing

01:36:29.036 --> 01:36:32.680
agent, the payroll, the recreation, the wildlife biologists. I'm not saying they're not

01:36:33.180 --> 01:36:35.580
going forward. I just want to focus on those if the rest are good.

01:36:35.640 --> 01:36:39.580
The wildlife biologist, I, I don't know that there's anyone on council that disputes.

01:36:40.280 --> 01:36:43.400
No, I'm not saying that. I'm saying there's been questions about it, so we're

01:36:43.900 --> 01:36:46.020
going to talk about those 5 if the rest of the list is good.

01:36:46.810 --> 01:36:50.540
But, but I think the wildlife biologist has enough support of

01:36:51.040 --> 01:36:54.180
at least 4 people. Sure. So I don't think so. That's, that's not the question.

01:36:54.680 --> 01:36:58.223
The question is Everything except those 5 are we good with? And I'm sorry,

01:36:58.239 --> 01:37:00.841
I'm not saying that right. I want to know if we can make— what we're

01:37:01.341 --> 01:37:05.162
saying, Mayor, is we're okay with— we're okay with only— we

01:37:05.662 --> 01:37:08.856
only want to talk about 4. That's what we're saying. Well, and can we— can

01:37:09.356 --> 01:37:13.177
we have Council Member Hewish weigh in on whether he meant purchasing agent or if

01:37:13.677 --> 01:37:17.385
he was talking about payroll and benefits administrator?

01:37:17.691 --> 01:37:20.341
Because they're right next to each other. He meant purchasing. Okay.

01:37:24.430 --> 01:37:27.590
Okay, so I just want to make sure if there's any others that we need

01:37:28.090 --> 01:37:30.730
to talk about. That's what I'm asking about. No, the whole rest of the list

01:37:31.230 --> 01:37:34.810
is good. Okay, that's what I want to know. I, I think, I think actually

01:37:35.310 --> 01:37:39.530
Council Member Hewish may have a comment on the arborist technician as well though.

01:37:39.650 --> 01:37:41.810
So he and I were talking about that one earlier and he had a question

01:37:42.310 --> 01:37:46.250
mark on that. Then, then that's what— then we need to ask what that position

01:37:46.750 --> 01:37:49.730
does, and that's what I'm curious. That's Let's ask about that one. Okay, then I'm

01:37:50.230 --> 01:37:53.613
going to put a question mark by that one. Now, if we're good, I'm going

01:37:54.113 --> 01:37:56.517
to start from the bottom, and I'm hearing— and I want to just say this—

01:37:57.046 --> 01:38:00.848
there is at least one council member that had some

01:38:00.912 --> 01:38:04.265
questions about wildlife biologists. But if 4 people are saying go with it,

01:38:04.281 --> 01:38:07.811
then I'm— then we're going to leave it on. Is that what— at least 2.

01:38:07.955 --> 01:38:11.437
Okay, we're good on that one. The— going from

01:38:11.469 --> 01:38:14.949
the bottom, Community Service Director, I've heard all over the board.

01:38:15.029 --> 01:38:18.266
Is that one you do want to advance or not advance, or do you

01:38:18.282 --> 01:38:21.311
need— or what specific questions you have on that one?

01:38:21.904 --> 01:38:24.708
Is that the— can Ben just speak to it? I mean, you're talking about a

01:38:25.208 --> 01:38:28.282
$200,000 a year position. What's the purpose of

01:38:28.394 --> 01:38:31.439
it? That's my question. Absolutely, I'd love to.

01:38:32.400 --> 01:38:35.958
So on this particular one, when we look at

01:38:36.086 --> 01:38:39.499
the three areas that we would be focusing on— quality of life,

01:38:39.708 --> 01:38:43.016
essential services and, and, um, uh,

01:38:43.289 --> 01:38:46.196
community and economic development.

01:38:46.678 --> 01:38:50.324
This is the quality of life one, and a good community services director

01:38:50.436 --> 01:38:53.873
will come in and end up working to pay for themselves

01:38:54.146 --> 01:38:58.001
with the development of new soccer programs, new programming all over the place.

01:38:59.125 --> 01:39:02.771
They will basically— they'll be the partner with the parks

01:39:02.980 --> 01:39:07.267
director who would be taking care of and maintaining it, but come

01:39:07.380 --> 01:39:10.559
at it with the programming. So a good community services director will

01:39:11.153 --> 01:39:14.621
help take care of your seniors, they'll be your— help take

01:39:15.121 --> 01:39:18.635
care of your sports and recreation, help take care of your events. They are

01:39:19.135 --> 01:39:23.196
the main pivot person between anything that is quality of life, library, and whatnot.

01:39:23.613 --> 01:39:27.531
And they'll be your main contact person with the community services board,

01:39:27.547 --> 01:39:29.442
your main staff person to that board.

01:39:30.903 --> 01:39:34.387
And I just looked at, you know,

01:39:35.029 --> 01:39:38.546
certain cities when they truly transform and bloom

01:39:38.626 --> 01:39:42.126
in the realm of quality of life and the service offerings that we give,

01:39:42.496 --> 01:39:46.012
it's because that community services director comes in, listens to the council,

01:39:46.269 --> 01:39:49.866
listens to the community, and starts developing the programming. And the programming

01:39:50.366 --> 01:39:54.024
themselves with all the user rates and fees and whatnot helps to pay each time

01:39:54.105 --> 01:39:57.139
you have one. There's a little bit of overhead that helps pay for that person.

01:39:57.593 --> 01:40:00.452
When we had RAP tax on there, it was really just to kick off that

01:40:00.484 --> 01:40:05.320
position to start off, but not be a long-term stream

01:40:05.368 --> 01:40:08.214
of paying for that. It would really just be the seed money to start it.

01:40:08.584 --> 01:40:12.079
But that's, that's the idea behind it. And if it's this year, great. If it's

01:40:12.579 --> 01:40:14.864
not, if it's in the future, that's great. It's just that the RAP tax was

01:40:15.364 --> 01:40:18.348
approved by the voters, and so there's a certain expectation in the community that we're

01:40:18.848 --> 01:40:22.046
going to make some quality of life improvements. So then this might help with

01:40:22.546 --> 01:40:25.897
council as well as I think one of the questions on

01:40:26.397 --> 01:40:29.906
that position and whether it's now or later is up to you

01:40:30.406 --> 01:40:33.850
guys. It's not important to me. What I want to know is one of the

01:40:34.350 --> 01:40:37.908
biggest questions is the expense. And I think that number came maybe

01:40:38.408 --> 01:40:41.387
from HR and it has to do with more areas with the rec center,

01:40:41.852 --> 01:40:45.124
which we don't have. Is it possible that that

01:40:45.624 --> 01:40:49.197
position could be hired and have some of those same responsibilities

01:40:49.245 --> 01:40:52.516
but at a, more reasonable expense

01:40:53.016 --> 01:40:56.443
being with less responsibility until we had a

01:40:56.908 --> 01:41:00.931
higher need. If that was the case, would that change the, the need

01:41:01.431 --> 01:41:04.394
sooner rather than later if it was for less money?

01:41:04.458 --> 01:41:07.889
So is that even a possibility, or has it just got to be this

01:41:08.389 --> 01:41:11.415
or we won't find anybody? Um, it really depends on what you want.

01:41:11.527 --> 01:41:14.781
So you bought— you pay for what you get and where you

01:41:14.797 --> 01:41:18.837
want to go. You can hire to maintain

01:41:19.337 --> 01:41:20.977
maintaining what is today. And we, in fact, are— we have fantastic staff. There's no

01:41:21.477 --> 01:41:24.815
doubt that they're doing a lot. They could do so much more if they had

01:41:25.315 --> 01:41:28.461
greater investment in our existing staff. But it's where you want to go.

01:41:28.847 --> 01:41:32.091
And so it's really a matter of timing. How fast do you want to

01:41:32.139 --> 01:41:35.512
take on recreational improvements? And if you want to wait on this one and make

01:41:36.012 --> 01:41:39.608
it a mid-year appointment, or consider it, you know, after you

01:41:40.108 --> 01:41:43.350
start seeing how much money is coming in with RAP tax and MET, nothing things

01:41:43.850 --> 01:41:47.027
like that, that's just fine. This is really all about timing and your

01:41:47.155 --> 01:41:50.607
desire as a council to raise the level of service and community and quality

01:41:51.107 --> 01:41:54.750
of life at the timing that you feel good about. Okay, so then

01:41:54.782 --> 01:41:58.122
what I'm looking for, and I'll let Councilmember Clark weigh in, is,

01:41:58.796 --> 01:42:02.232
is it now or later and at what expense? Because that, that's kind

01:42:02.732 --> 01:42:04.255
of what we want to know. Do you want to do it cheaper? Do you

01:42:04.755 --> 01:42:08.430
want to fund the full amount now, later, whatever? So Councilmember Clark, I think that

01:42:08.930 --> 01:42:12.396
we should wait and I've shared some of the reasons because I think we need

01:42:12.896 --> 01:42:14.837
to take care of the boots on the ground that are currently doing that.

01:42:15.318 --> 01:42:18.530
Library, rec, events, you know,

01:42:18.562 --> 01:42:22.367
the major things right there, seniors, they're all being

01:42:22.640 --> 01:42:26.076
done right now by current employees. I think that we

01:42:26.576 --> 01:42:29.753
need to fund those employees so that they can

01:42:29.866 --> 01:42:33.414
continue to grow the program, and a year from now when we're

01:42:33.462 --> 01:42:37.236
having this discussion And again, we'll ask ourselves, are we ready to

01:42:37.252 --> 01:42:40.848
bring in a middle manager to help coordinate that and sit in

01:42:40.945 --> 01:42:44.397
budget meetings and do all the boring parts of administration?

01:42:45.120 --> 01:42:48.636
Because right now what I care about is what the people see, and that's the

01:42:49.136 --> 01:42:52.168
events, the rec, the library, all of those things. And I think we need to

01:42:52.668 --> 01:42:56.102
take care of the people who are doing the job, and then we

01:42:56.602 --> 01:42:59.795
look at bringing in someone who coordinates the people doing the job.

01:42:59.956 --> 01:43:03.404
That's my opinion on this. I'm gonna wait.

01:43:04.644 --> 01:43:08.816
I'd rather wait on it. And I also am questioning the $147,000

01:43:09.316 --> 01:43:12.994
salary. I think we can get a high-quality person for something less

01:43:13.494 --> 01:43:16.946
than that number, especially when I'm looking at that and it's being proposed

01:43:17.446 --> 01:43:21.192
to be $27,000 more than an attorney, which takes

01:43:21.241 --> 01:43:25.447
a lot of years of schooling, et cetera, to do. So I

01:43:25.512 --> 01:43:27.599
would question the salary on that one.

01:43:29.130 --> 01:43:31.967
Okay, I think some of that, and we could speak to it, is it actually

01:43:31.999 --> 01:43:35.542
included a car, um, and I don't know where that came into

01:43:35.606 --> 01:43:38.941
the number. So it, it doesn't come into— that's a separate number.

01:43:39.694 --> 01:43:43.541
I would like to add, only from where HR kind of falls under administrative

01:43:44.041 --> 01:43:47.549
services, from just a context with some things, this particular

01:43:48.049 --> 01:43:51.669
position in terms of a community service director is a functional area director position.

01:43:52.535 --> 01:43:56.790
It's not a middle management position. That's why you're seeing a cost

01:43:57.290 --> 01:44:00.130
associated with that in terms of what this role would be. But don't we already

01:44:00.630 --> 01:44:04.160
pretty top-heavy in functional area directors? I mean, that's my concern.

01:44:04.470 --> 01:44:07.810
I would say— okay, my response to that is going to be no.

01:44:08.350 --> 01:44:10.990
And I'm going to just talk to you a little bit about why from my

01:44:11.490 --> 01:44:14.310
perspective where that means and what no means and when we talk about what a

01:44:14.810 --> 01:44:18.750
functional area like director is doing and what that involvement is.

01:44:19.250 --> 01:44:22.369
So What we're hearing from the council is that we want to heighten,

01:44:23.275 --> 01:44:27.059
right, we, we want to enhance this area that we have. And Community Services

01:44:27.091 --> 01:44:30.651
is an area that, again, that we don't have right

01:44:31.151 --> 01:44:34.940
now. There's not a functional area director. Uh, Steve is functioning in that capacity,

01:44:35.021 --> 01:44:37.966
taking that on in regards to what his role is as well.

01:44:39.530 --> 01:44:42.740
One of the challenges that Eagle Mountain has historically had is

01:44:42.756 --> 01:44:45.928
the exact conversation that we're having. We need to We

01:44:46.428 --> 01:44:48.433
want to grow, we want to enhance, but we don't want to pay for it.

01:44:48.979 --> 01:44:52.046
We want to bring somebody else in to be able to do it and hope

01:44:52.062 --> 01:44:56.124
that they can grow as our organization grows. If we were starting to

01:44:56.156 --> 01:44:59.416
try to develop a public safety or police department, we wouldn't start by

01:44:59.916 --> 01:45:03.141
hiring a sergeant or a police officer or whatever.

01:45:03.912 --> 01:45:07.508
If— again, this is just my personal recommendation coming

01:45:07.845 --> 01:45:11.554
from the capacity that I sit in and where I am at. That if community

01:45:12.054 --> 01:45:14.713
services is an area that you want to enhance,

01:45:15.082 --> 01:45:18.963
that leadership role is a priority position that needs to be filled

01:45:19.573 --> 01:45:23.759
in order to grow and develop that. And I'm not saying that

01:45:24.259 --> 01:45:26.999
in any way, shape, or form of choosing this over enhancing.

01:45:27.913 --> 01:45:31.441
As you're aware, we do have a compensation study that is just getting ready to

01:45:31.941 --> 01:45:35.723
kick off and get underway that is going to look at our structure

01:45:35.772 --> 01:45:39.428
and our compensation to our employees and the positions and the responsibilities

01:45:39.460 --> 01:45:42.255
and the things that they're looking at. I'm gonna be careful that this isn't a,

01:45:42.271 --> 01:45:46.191
we have to choose this over something else, and just

01:45:46.691 --> 01:45:49.982
look at this from what is the purpose of a community service director, and what

01:45:50.482 --> 01:45:53.660
would they be bringing to our organization? And I do completely look at it from

01:45:53.741 --> 01:45:57.676
a leadership standpoint, and having that to help fit

01:45:58.176 --> 01:46:01.997
in, so. I totally understand where you're coming from, but the reality

01:46:02.030 --> 01:46:05.805
of it is, is their role would be to grow programs like events,

01:46:05.821 --> 01:46:09.546
like soccer, like all of those things. And if we aren't in a position

01:46:09.899 --> 01:46:13.527
where we can fund enough people to take care of the fields that

01:46:14.027 --> 01:46:17.268
soccer will be played on or do some of those other things, then it doesn't

01:46:17.284 --> 01:46:20.880
make sense to bring in someone to do that

01:46:21.380 --> 01:46:25.086
job until we get that baseline of level of service with

01:46:25.586 --> 01:46:29.003
our amenities, I think, improved. The last thing I'm going to say

01:46:29.503 --> 01:46:33.177
about it is that largely the things that would be

01:46:33.273 --> 01:46:37.154
done by the community services director are going to take place during

01:46:37.490 --> 01:46:40.775
the summer. That's where we're going to see the biggest, like, bang for

01:46:41.275 --> 01:46:44.845
our buck. It's the things that the residents will

01:46:44.877 --> 01:46:48.194
see because we want them to see it. If we

01:46:48.307 --> 01:46:51.752
go out to hire someone, we won't be bringing them on

01:46:52.252 --> 01:46:55.469
and starting them probably until at least August, if not September.

01:46:56.719 --> 01:47:00.693
And when we do that, all of the really cool big events

01:47:00.902 --> 01:47:04.196
the rec, like all of those things will have taken place. And the

01:47:04.276 --> 01:47:07.811
quote unquote return on investment of putting the money into a

01:47:08.311 --> 01:47:12.326
community services director could be immediately seen if

01:47:12.391 --> 01:47:15.813
we enhance the salaries of the people who are currently

01:47:15.861 --> 01:47:19.396
doing the jobs. I'm not a no on this. I'm a wait

01:47:19.896 --> 01:47:23.317
until we take care of some of our other bigger issues. Okay,

01:47:23.397 --> 01:47:26.675
perfect. And just to be clear, none of these positions will be before September.

01:47:27.325 --> 01:47:30.973
They all have to wait. So— Actually, yes. Yeah,

01:47:31.022 --> 01:47:34.557
that's correct. So the earliest

01:47:34.573 --> 01:47:38.220
any of these would be is September. And just

01:47:38.720 --> 01:47:41.705
real quick, I'm trying to get to some of these other ones, but if we

01:47:42.205 --> 01:47:45.384
didn't fund that one, that duty falls to Steve.

01:47:45.819 --> 01:47:49.274
Steve, I just want to hear that you're

01:47:49.323 --> 01:47:52.752
capable and that we can get by and that— You know,

01:47:52.784 --> 01:47:56.195
I mean, your chance to say that you're

01:47:56.388 --> 01:47:59.542
not overworked. I'll never say

01:48:03.448 --> 01:48:06.216
I just want them to understand what they're saying no to, right?

01:48:06.769 --> 01:48:09.212
And what you do to make up for the difference.

01:48:09.950 --> 01:48:13.334
Yeah, I appreciate that. I think really what this decision does

01:48:13.672 --> 01:48:17.121
is impact Ben more than anything because then

01:48:17.234 --> 01:48:20.383
I spend more of my time on community services and less time helping him with

01:48:20.883 --> 01:48:23.562
administration. So, but am I capable?

01:48:23.755 --> 01:48:28.154
Absolutely. It just comes down to balance of helping

01:48:28.571 --> 01:48:32.264
Brandon with community development, helping with community

01:48:32.764 --> 01:48:36.985
services, and whatever else Ben has me doing.

01:48:38.366 --> 01:48:41.593
Okay, I just wanted to hear it, and if they change their mind, they can

01:48:42.093 --> 01:48:45.349
let us know. Let's move up to the— Actually, I'm going to jump to the

01:48:45.849 --> 01:48:48.810
top of the list just in case we run out of time. But the assistant

01:48:49.310 --> 01:48:52.401
city attorney, I've heard a few people say pin.

01:48:52.578 --> 01:48:55.494
I don't know that everybody said that. Is that the general consensus?

01:48:57.986 --> 01:49:02.095
Wait. Melissa? I would say wait and use

01:49:02.225 --> 01:49:06.180
outsourcing non-permanent resources to make

01:49:06.680 --> 01:49:09.821
that happen. Then in a 30-second synopsis,

01:49:10.128 --> 01:49:13.718
Marcus, if you want to let us know why we either

01:49:14.218 --> 01:49:16.399
do need it or what it will do if we don't fund it. Like,

01:49:16.480 --> 01:49:19.469
what are we going to miss? Sure.

01:49:19.923 --> 01:49:23.099
I just want to kind of very briefly address something that

01:49:23.599 --> 01:49:29.698
Councilmember Wright said earlier. The reason that we have right now a lot of outside

01:49:29.714 --> 01:49:33.072
attorney fees is because we have a lot of litigation going on.

01:49:33.587 --> 01:49:37.290
So that's the reason. That's not always been the case. Which has been the case

01:49:37.790 --> 01:49:41.410
recently. As far as Councilmember Clark's statement,

01:49:41.910 --> 01:49:45.310
I would say that outsourcing it is

01:49:45.810 --> 01:49:49.080
significantly more expensive, and we would go— we would plow through that.

01:49:49.330 --> 01:49:52.550
If we're talking about funding a position, say, just use a round figure,

01:49:53.050 --> 01:49:57.850
$100,000 a year, tack on another 40% for benefits.

01:49:58.350 --> 01:50:01.710
You know, if you're paying, you know, $400 an hour for an outside attorney,

01:50:01.812 --> 01:50:04.512
handle that. You plow through that pretty quickly.

01:50:04.818 --> 01:50:08.371
So that's the downside to doing that, is you get significantly

01:50:08.403 --> 01:50:12.020
less bang for your buck to use outside counsel on those

01:50:12.520 --> 01:50:15.574
situations. The goal there would be to, one,

01:50:16.106 --> 01:50:19.584
you know, per city code, attorneys should be attending DRC

01:50:19.633 --> 01:50:22.854
every week. That's one thing that I just don't have bandwidth to do. So there's

01:50:23.354 --> 01:50:29.164
just certain responsibilities that fall by the wayside. And You

01:50:29.196 --> 01:50:31.521
know, that this person would be able to, I think,

01:50:32.086 --> 01:50:35.230
handle some more of those community development responsibilities, I think,

01:50:36.139 --> 01:50:39.772
where they would be able to spend more time with the ordinances earlier in the

01:50:39.853 --> 01:50:44.160
process, spend more time, you know, assisting earlier

01:50:44.660 --> 01:50:47.267
in the process rather than waiting for people to say, "I need help with this,"

01:50:48.829 --> 01:50:52.165
and trying— and just faster timelines in getting things done.

01:50:54.335 --> 01:50:57.097
That would be, I guess, the 30-second version. I'm sure I could go deeper into

01:50:57.145 --> 01:51:00.276
it if I had more

01:51:00.776 --> 01:51:04.820
time. It would be interesting for the council to probably learn

01:51:04.917 --> 01:51:08.465
how much we have spent on outside counsel in the last 12 months.

01:51:09.268 --> 01:51:13.105
And it would also be interesting to learn whether we can hire— like, because part

01:51:13.605 --> 01:51:16.542
of the outsourcing is because of the specialties involved with outsourcing.

01:51:17.457 --> 01:51:20.764
And so— Can we hire someone that could

01:51:21.433 --> 01:51:24.188
pull that outsourcing back in, or are we gonna still be outsourcing?

01:51:24.946 --> 01:51:28.200
And so it'd be interesting to hear, here's how much money we spent on outside

01:51:28.700 --> 01:51:31.925
counsel, here's how much was for specialized outside counsel,

01:51:32.562 --> 01:51:35.977
and that could help us better understand the situation.

01:51:39.073 --> 01:51:43.118
I would say the majority of our attorney's fees are just general litigation

01:51:43.378 --> 01:51:47.149
right now. We have one case I think would be specialized,

01:51:47.229 --> 01:51:49.797
more specialized outside counsel that we would still probably,

01:51:52.396 --> 01:51:55.590
you know, farm out. And then the other cases are

01:51:55.639 --> 01:51:58.344
the types of cases that if we had, you know,

01:51:58.376 --> 01:52:01.660
litigator and like a legal secretary or paralegal,

01:52:01.918 --> 01:52:06.490
that we could handle in-house. But we can't currently, because— Marcus,

01:52:06.506 --> 01:52:09.550
can you speak to some of the things that perhaps you're not able to get

01:52:10.050 --> 01:52:12.616
to, because you just don't have the bandwidth? I heard you say DRC. I mean,

01:52:12.648 --> 01:52:13.669
DRC is one example.

01:52:17.859 --> 01:52:21.752
I think, you know, it just, you know, like reviewing ordinances

01:52:21.784 --> 01:52:24.550
would happen more quickly would be an example.

01:52:27.609 --> 01:52:31.398
You know, right now I have, you know, Comcast is up for renewal

01:52:31.415 --> 01:52:35.091
on a franchise agreement, and that's just kind of been on the back burner because

01:52:36.867 --> 01:52:40.239
just the other responsibilities. And so it's more of a timeline

01:52:40.739 --> 01:52:43.354
thing, and getting things done more quickly.

01:52:44.767 --> 01:52:48.043
Litigation, I think, is the perfect example. I would want to hire

01:52:48.543 --> 01:52:52.458
somebody that had litigation experience, because I do think that is a way to help

01:52:52.958 --> 01:52:56.777
the position pay for itself, because right now that is specifically something I've just

01:52:57.277 --> 01:53:00.888
told city council members time and again, I don't have bandwidth, you know, for things

01:53:01.388 --> 01:53:05.336
like that. So I think that's probably

01:53:05.836 --> 01:53:10.008
the biggest example of something that's just not getting done.

01:53:11.244 --> 01:53:15.291
But yeah. So I mean, I'm going

01:53:15.791 --> 01:53:19.353
to reserve the right to change my mind, okay? Because I

01:53:19.853 --> 01:53:22.452
know, look, I wanted to know why do we need an assistant city attorney.

01:53:22.885 --> 01:53:26.466
I sit in those DRC meetings, I listen to those conversations, and there's

01:53:26.498 --> 01:53:29.743
never a meeting that happens happens there that I don't hear somebody say,

01:53:30.131 --> 01:53:33.868
"I'll get with our legal counsel and I'll get back to you," right? And I

01:53:33.933 --> 01:53:36.036
see that happen every Thursday.

01:53:38.667 --> 01:53:40.970
So you're giving me pause on this here.

01:53:43.483 --> 01:53:46.685
Because we went a number of years without an attorney and,

01:53:47.023 --> 01:53:50.450
well, we were with a part-time attorney, which was almost being

01:53:50.482 --> 01:53:53.940
without an attorney, if I'm being frank. And I just,

01:53:53.956 --> 01:53:57.297
I think we've been much better served having full-time legal counsel

01:53:57.812 --> 01:54:00.671
on staff. And I'm probably,

01:54:01.330 --> 01:54:03.852
I think I'm moving in a place where I'm going to feel okay about,

01:54:04.077 --> 01:54:07.387
about saying yes to this. Marcus, really quick,

01:54:07.676 --> 01:54:11.371
um, with this position, I think that's when I say

01:54:11.452 --> 01:54:14.087
outsourcing. For two decades,

01:54:14.585 --> 01:54:18.119
we at least had a city attorney that

01:54:18.619 --> 01:54:21.717
was a part of a firm who was quote unquote our city attorney,

01:54:22.215 --> 01:54:25.747
but it was that. So part of me is thinking, do we need another

01:54:26.247 --> 01:54:30.082
full-time person, or could we do something like

01:54:30.098 --> 01:54:34.016
that arrangement, full-time person plus that contracted

01:54:34.516 --> 01:54:38.095
type person? Or could we also stretch your reach

01:54:38.592 --> 01:54:42.221
if we had a legal secretary or a paralegal or something like

01:54:42.721 --> 01:54:46.589
that that's maybe less costly but allows allows us to get

01:54:46.638 --> 01:54:50.349
more help for you, because like a lot of what you do is also

01:54:50.638 --> 01:54:54.350
the busy work of law, and like reviewing the contract and

01:54:54.850 --> 01:54:57.515
stuff like that, and I'm just wondering, could we get more bang for our buck

01:54:57.563 --> 01:55:01.371
if we had a busy work paralegal

01:55:01.564 --> 01:55:05.447
that could help? Like, so I think we're just looking and asking about

01:55:05.511 --> 01:55:06.371
options. Sure.

01:55:08.786 --> 01:55:12.271
So I guess, you know, addressing kind of like the part-time, if you're envisioning like

01:55:12.771 --> 01:55:16.429
a part-time staff attorney. My experiences with that have been

01:55:16.929 --> 01:55:20.459
negative. When I worked for Provo, we did have part-time attorneys in that office,

01:55:20.829 --> 01:55:24.185
and it was extremely difficult to find qualified individuals that wanted part-time

01:55:24.685 --> 01:55:28.600
work. It happens at times, but other times you get

01:55:29.100 --> 01:55:32.550
less qualified applicants, and it was just really hard to keep those filled and to

01:55:33.050 --> 01:55:36.291
get good people in. So that's just kind of my personal experience, and so I'm

01:55:36.323 --> 01:55:39.111
not a huge fan of that route. For that reason.

01:55:40.720 --> 01:55:43.469
As far as a, you know, paralegal, legal secretary,

01:55:45.102 --> 01:55:48.579
where I see the greatest value in a position

01:55:49.079 --> 01:55:52.135
like that is, yes, that person could do some of the

01:55:52.151 --> 01:55:55.819
busy work of that I—

01:55:57.080 --> 01:56:00.752
I mean, for want of a better term, that I do, like in running a

01:56:01.252 --> 01:56:05.148
department and a budget and all that. Could they help assist

01:56:05.648 --> 01:56:09.043
the to free me up for the stuff that I'm actually, you know,

01:56:09.059 --> 01:56:12.226
that I'm primarily getting paid for, if you want to put it that way.

01:56:15.991 --> 01:56:17.838
Sure.

01:56:18.722 --> 01:56:22.305
That's helpful. You know, a lot of,

01:56:22.642 --> 01:56:25.408
you know, I don't know that I spend so much of my time doing things

01:56:25.908 --> 01:56:29.703
like that. Okay. Final question. If we fund

01:56:29.735 --> 01:56:33.220
an assistant city attorney, Will we finally get our justice court?

01:56:34.640 --> 01:56:37.600
You know, you can— I think we're entitled to a justice court. There's just a

01:56:38.100 --> 01:56:41.480
process we have to follow. If you want a justice court— Don't answer it like

01:56:41.980 --> 01:56:44.880
a lawyer. Answer it like somebody who's in a budget meeting. I mean, we are

01:56:45.380 --> 01:56:49.000
legally entitled to one. It's just a process. Oh my gosh. We are legally

01:56:49.500 --> 01:56:52.700
entitled to one. Yes. I got

01:56:53.200 --> 01:56:56.460
to keep moving. I got to keep moving. So look,

01:56:56.960 --> 01:57:00.516
we have planning commissions in 9:30, is that right? I know we started just a

01:57:01.016 --> 01:57:03.920
couple minutes late, so I know if it's okay, we're going to go over just

01:57:04.420 --> 01:57:07.901
a little bit, but we have got 3 or 4 more things. First of all,

01:57:07.982 --> 01:57:11.771
I want to jump off the list for one second. I read the

01:57:11.835 --> 01:57:16.235
property tax impact schedule in the beginning of the meeting, and really

01:57:16.267 --> 01:57:19.285
we need to know, or I'm kind of curious, that it has

01:57:19.301 --> 01:57:23.203
to do with some of this, the direction. Is the council proposing

01:57:23.828 --> 01:57:27.482
the 245% or what's

01:57:28.219 --> 01:57:31.520
the— is it— it depends on what— like,

01:57:31.825 --> 01:57:34.965
I need to know. You proposed it, Mayor. We just get to vote on it

01:57:35.094 --> 01:57:38.940
and adjust it. Okay, I don't

01:57:39.440 --> 01:57:42.673
even know if that's a fair statement, but I'll accept it and say, are you

01:57:42.721 --> 01:57:45.606
accepting the proposal or what do you want to do with that number?

01:57:45.910 --> 01:57:49.404
I think the people of Eagle Mountain want us to make sure

01:57:49.904 --> 01:57:53.402
do all the due diligence. And I also think we're all very sensitive

01:57:53.902 --> 01:57:57.288
to the fact that even if we vote yes and propose this and move it

01:57:57.384 --> 01:58:00.467
forward, that there are other things that can happen to act

01:58:00.967 --> 01:58:04.176
upon us that make it so that that doesn't necessarily pan out.

01:58:04.369 --> 01:58:08.062
And I think all of us are looking at that potential

01:58:08.319 --> 01:58:11.595
and the whole if-come thing and saying we're

01:58:12.095 --> 01:58:16.324
hesitant to fully go all out with some of these positions

01:58:16.356 --> 01:58:19.812
because of that concern? I'm going to say I'm unprepared to answer

01:58:20.312 --> 01:58:23.687
that question, Mayor, until we have our public hearings and we listen to the public

01:58:24.187 --> 01:58:27.538
and we take that input. I mean, we wouldn't be—

01:58:27.554 --> 01:58:30.857
we wouldn't be honest if we wouldn't— if we wouldn't at least own the

01:58:31.357 --> 01:58:34.627
fact that we're considering it. But that's where it's at for me

01:58:35.127 --> 01:58:38.374
right now currently, is that I'm considering it. I think it's needful.

01:58:40.593 --> 01:58:44.286
But certainly I've not drawn a conclusion that that's absolutely where I'm

01:58:44.786 --> 01:58:47.658
going to vote. I want to see what the people have to say. Mayor,

01:58:47.722 --> 01:58:51.575
exactly what Councilmember Wright said. Can you keep going? So June

01:58:52.075 --> 01:58:55.718
16th meeting is where we do have to have the council

01:58:55.991 --> 01:58:59.475
make a decision that you're— this is what is being recommended

01:58:59.975 --> 01:59:04.164
as the proposed going forward. That's the— we have to do that because

01:59:04.180 --> 01:59:07.616
we have to certify our stuff by June $1.2 million. That doesn't mean

01:59:07.728 --> 01:59:11.967
that is what the council adopts. It means this is what is the proposed amount

01:59:12.467 --> 01:59:14.874
that goes on the tax statements and for the hearing and for all of the

01:59:15.374 --> 01:59:18.807
notices. But that decision will have to be made on the June 16th

01:59:19.307 --> 01:59:22.131
meeting along with the interim budget. So are you saying that you need us to

01:59:22.179 --> 01:59:25.680
say that? That's— I'm saying you don't have to say that today, but you have

01:59:25.712 --> 01:59:29.068
to be prepared for June 16th. Yes. Okay, that's good.

01:59:29.148 --> 01:59:32.263
That's great, Council. Thank you. So it'll be prior to the public hearing. And the

01:59:32.279 --> 01:59:36.695
reason why I jumped to that is because if

01:59:36.791 --> 01:59:40.580
there's no appetite to go to whatever, then we

01:59:41.080 --> 01:59:44.016
have to have a deeper conversation over here. That's why I kind of jumped back.

01:59:44.514 --> 01:59:47.870
So I just wanted to make sure that we're on the

01:59:48.370 --> 01:59:50.921
same page there. And if that's the case, go ahead. Can we have Melissa come

01:59:51.421 --> 01:59:54.710
up and talk about the purchasing agent? That's where— that, and then right

01:59:55.210 --> 01:59:59.360
after that, the human resources, uh, And just for some information too, so this purchasing

01:59:59.860 --> 02:00:03.713
agent position is actually a request that started last fiscal year. This was a last

02:00:04.213 --> 02:00:07.715
fiscal year request that was on hold. It was reconsidered again

02:00:07.763 --> 02:00:11.203
mid-year, but again, mid-year is your— where we didn't have additional resources that we

02:00:11.703 --> 02:00:14.916
could put towards. We didn't move anything forward, and it is continuing to

02:00:15.384 --> 02:00:18.529
pulse forward, and it's a high demand and need.

02:00:20.671 --> 02:00:24.143
I'm grateful to be here. Purchasing agent

02:00:24.643 --> 02:00:28.893
generally goes a little overlooked. Every purchase

02:00:29.393 --> 02:00:33.086
over $75,000 comes through me. Every contract that we do comes through

02:00:33.586 --> 02:00:37.404
me. I write them. If we have red lines, Marcus approves them and we shuffle

02:00:37.517 --> 02:00:41.605
them out. I'm very busy. I work

02:00:42.060 --> 02:00:43.197
overtime every week.

02:00:46.378 --> 02:00:49.006
It's difficult. To get the job done.

02:00:49.851 --> 02:00:52.971
And I'm teary because I care about the

02:00:53.471 --> 02:00:57.696
city and I care about my departments. It's important

02:00:58.196 --> 02:01:02.425
to me that they get their contracts in a timely manner. It's important

02:01:02.925 --> 02:01:10.208
to me that RFPs get put out and done correctly and that

02:01:10.257 --> 02:01:13.944
vendors are taken care of. I take care of all your vendors.

02:01:14.073 --> 02:01:16.183
I take care of all your bids. I take care of all your contracts.

02:01:17.541 --> 02:01:21.399
I take care of all the POs that come in, over 5,000.

02:01:21.447 --> 02:01:25.352
My name's on all of them. I do all your change orders. I do anything

02:01:25.368 --> 02:01:29.290
financially as far as purchasing comes

02:01:29.403 --> 02:01:33.389
through me. We are a growing city. We are growing exponentially.

02:01:34.063 --> 02:01:35.839
I cannot keep up.

02:01:37.292 --> 02:01:40.901
At this point, and I apologize

02:01:40.934 --> 02:01:43.120
again, I'm sorry for being emotional.

02:01:45.193 --> 02:01:47.412
I cannot keep up with the work that's coming through.

02:01:49.000 --> 02:01:53.605
I need help. That's just the end point. And Marcus

02:01:53.735 --> 02:01:57.082
knows because I'm shuffling him red lines and negotiation

02:01:57.114 --> 02:02:00.374
stuff all the time, and he needs help. We cannot keep

02:02:00.874 --> 02:02:04.765
up with the contracts that are coming through. We just cannot. Largely, I'm doing,

02:02:05.024 --> 02:02:08.446
um, most of the general contract management while Marcus is

02:02:09.088 --> 02:02:12.906
handling all the upper stuff. So when we go through the negotiations

02:02:13.406 --> 02:02:16.679
on my side, I'm shuffling him all red lines and we're backed up.

02:02:18.087 --> 02:02:21.938
That's just it. I need help to keep the city moving.

02:02:24.222 --> 02:02:27.427
Questions for me? I really just wanted— Melissa, for you,

02:02:27.750 --> 02:02:31.326
I sense that, and I'm in favor of this position, by the way. Could you

02:02:31.826 --> 02:02:35.017
just give us an idea of how many contracts and how many RFPs you're dealing

02:02:35.115 --> 02:02:38.748
with, perhaps? On a weekly or monthly basis? I think that might help us understand

02:02:39.248 --> 02:02:40.516
the volume or the workload that you're dealing with.

02:02:42.598 --> 02:02:45.964
Since May— well, no, that's a lie.

02:02:47.193 --> 02:02:51.287
The end of March until now, I've done

02:02:51.724 --> 02:02:54.367
10 RFPs. I have a bunch in the hopper.

02:02:56.222 --> 02:02:59.827
That RFP means I do the worksheet with the

02:03:00.327 --> 02:03:03.739
